# Release Notes

## Unreleased

### New Features

- **After creating a team in the Flow Editor, the confirmation now takes you straight to Agent Chat, on the team's first agent** — the confirmation's **Open team** button used to lead back to the team's canvas in the Flow Editor, the very place you had just come from, when the next thing you want is to start talking to the new agents. It is now **Open in Agent Chat**: it closes the dialog exactly as before and opens Agent Chat with the chat of the team's first agent (the one created from the first role in the flow) already selected, its team group expanded and scrolled into view, even if a list filter or a collapsed group would otherwise have hidden it. If the new team has not reached the chat list yet, Agent Chat waits for it (briefly, then falls back to the normal overview) rather than jumping to a different agent. A team created without provisioning its agents lands on that first agent in its usual "Not initialised" state, with a hint to Materialize it, not an error. There is no longer a button back to the canvas. Applies to both the draft toolbar and the imported-bundle preview. (#2521)
- **The Skills Registry list now shows, at a glance, when a skill has out-of-date downstream copies** — until now, seeing that a publish/promote had left agents or teams on stale content meant opening that one skill's own detail view and clicking into its Usage tab, one skill at a time; the list's own "Drift" column only ever reflects a row's drift against *its own* upstream, so a global-tier row (the top of its own tree) always showed "—" there even right after making every copy below it outdated. A pool or team row now gets a **Downstream** column badge — e.g. "2 outdated, 1 diverged" — whenever one or more of its downstream copies (a pool row's team + agent copies; a team row's own agent copies) aren't `in-sync`, computed from the same per-copy drift already read for the existing Drift column (no new classification logic, so it can never disagree with the detail view) and folded in during the same pass instead of a per-skill lineage fetch. A row with no downstream copies, or all of them in sync, shows no badge — unchanged from today. Clicking the badge opens that skill's detail view straight on the Usage tab. (#2317)
- **You can now link straight to one specific chat message** — hovering a message in Agent Chat reveals a small link icon next to its timestamp; clicking it copies a `/chats/:agentId?msg=<id>` URL. Opening that link jumps to the right agent's chat, fetches older history if needed to locate the message (bounded, so a stale or bad link can't fetch forever), scrolls to it and briefly highlights it. If the message can't be found, the chat opens normally with a brief "Linked message not found" notice instead of an error. The link composes with the existing `?session=`/`?tab=` deep-links. (#2310)
- **The Agent Chat left rail now updates itself live instead of needing a manual reload** — the rail's roster (`GET /api/matrix/agents`) used to be fetched exactly once, so a pool instance spawned by autoscaling, a rename, an enable/disable toggle, or a removal made in another tab or by another user never showed up until you navigated away and back or reloaded the page. The rail now polls the same endpoint in the background every ~5s (one shared poll per open tab, paused while the tab isn't visible, one immediate refresh when it becomes visible again) and only re-renders the rows that actually changed — your selection, scroll position, group/instance collapse state, custom drag order, and any open chat's transcript are all left untouched by a background update. If the agent you're currently chatting with disappears from the roster (e.g. its pool instance is reaped), the conversation stays on screen with an inline notice instead of bouncing you to the empty state. (#2311)

- **A new "Scaling" section on a role/agent lets a single team member opt into pooling, without hand-editing a bundle or a raw API call** — a role's `pool.enabled`/`maxInstances`/workspace binding/teardown timeouts previously had no UI, only the live write route (#2270) they'd have to be `PUT` to by hand. A team member's node in the Flow editor's NodeInspector, and the same member's own Agent Chat **Settings** tab, now show a **Scaling** section: "This role auto-scales," max parallel instances (blank = no per-role cap, the team's ticket limit governs), a workspace binding picker, and idle/hard/retain-logs timeouts in minutes. Turning it on without the parent team's own ticket limit set is explained inline instead of failing with a raw error, pointing at the team's Parallelism panel. A pooled role also gets a read-only "pooled role · up to N" badge in the NodeInspector so its scaling is visible at a glance without opening the section. (#2271)
- **A new "Parallelism" panel in the Flow editor configures how many tickets a team works on at once, without hand-editing a bundle or a raw API call** — agent pools and the Flow Supervisor (auto-dispatch) previously had no UI at all: turning either on meant a hand-authored process-template bundle or a direct `PUT`. Selecting a live team in the Flow editor now shows a **Parallelism** toolbar button opening a panel with the ticket limit ("Up to N tickets at the same time"), an "Auto-start ready tickets" toggle, and a contention-strategy picker ("Serialize same epic," today's only enforced rule; "Full parallelism" and the LLM-based "Smart" predictor are shown for context but stay disabled — neither is wired up yet). Turning on auto-dispatch without a ticket limit or a pooled team member is explained inline instead of failing with a raw error. The team's own config page also gets a compact read-only summary of the same settings. (#2272)
- **An agent's container now starts with its Team Admin's own GitHub token and SSH key when they have one, instead of always the team's shared secret** — the token and key baked into a container at spawn (what a `gh`/`git` call the agent runs directly, such as commenting on an issue or reviewing a pull request, authenticates with) used to come straight from the environment's configured GitHub Token and SSH key fields, unconditionally shared by every agent in that environment. They now resolve through the same personal-credential preference commits, pull requests and pushes already use: if the team's current Team Admin has added a personal GitHub token or SSH key, every team-bound agent starts using it on its **next spawn** — no environment edit, no redeploy. An environment whose admin has never added a personal credential is completely unaffected. A container spawn never refuses over a missing personal credential, unlike an interactive write — a team with no personal credentials set up keeps working exactly as before. (#2177)
- **The general-purpose Working Environments page is gone — a team edits its own environment from Agent Chat, and a System Admin curates a shared Environment Templates catalog** — the old page served two audiences at once: every team editing its own infrastructure, and a System Admin maintaining shared, reusable ones. `/environments` is retired; an ordinary team now edits its environment without leaving Agent Chat, from the **Settings** tab's **Working Environment** section (unchanged except it gains the **GitHub Token** field, which had nowhere else to live once the standalone page went). A System Admin instead curates **Environment Templates** (`/environment-templates`), a catalog of environments marked as **templates** — a *copy source*, never a live-shared binding: no agent or team can ever be bound to one directly, and creating a team from one **copies its structure** (repositories, MCP pairings, model provider, toolchain, additional context) into a brand-new, independent environment, stripping every credential reference so the new team always supplies its own. This replaces the earlier **Global environment** toggle, which made a record directly usable by every team with no admin action at all — a pre-existing global environment is read as a template on the new catalog and becomes a real template record the first time it is saved there. (#2170)

- **The Session Log's Input/Result sections for most tool calls no longer show a raw JSON dump** — `Bash`, `Read`, `Grep`, `Glob`, `Write` and any other tool without its own bespoke render (unlike `Edit`'s red/green diff or a task-tracking tool's checklist) used to print `JSON.stringify(..., null, 2)` inside a `<pre>`: quoted keys, an escaped `\n` reading as two literal characters instead of a line break, and no visual structure for nested values. Both sections now render a pretty-printed key/value list instead — unquoted `key:` labels one per line, nested objects/arrays indented hierarchically, a multi-line string shown with real line breaks, and a markdown-like Result string (e.g. a `Bash` result's stdout) rendered through the same `SafeMarkdown` component chat messages use — a tool's Input (e.g. a `Bash` command) always stays literal/preformatted, even one that happens to look markdown-like (a shell `#` comment, a `**glob**`-shaped token), since it's the literal text the agent supplied, not prose to reinterpret. (#2197)

- **Creating a team on a platform with no model provider is now blocked with a clear message instead of succeeding and failing on first run** — a brand-new stack (or one where every provider has since been removed) used to let team creation go ahead regardless, silently binding the new environment to no provider at all; the team's first agent invocation was where that actually surfaced, as an opaque failure with no link back to the missing configuration. Both team-creation routes now refuse with "No model provider is configured for this platform yet. Ask a Team or System Administrator to add one under 'Model Providers' before creating a team." the moment the provider registry is empty, and the Team-Setup wizard and the Flow-Canvas **Create team** dialog disable their submit button with the same reason rather than letting the request reach the server at all. Once at least one provider exists, creation proceeds exactly as before. (#2179)
- **A shared agent's commit, PR or push now runs under the credentials of whoever is actually chatting, not whoever created the team** — until now, every attributed write from a shared agent ran under the team's creator, no matter which of the team's humans typed the message; two people sharing one agent could never tell their commits apart. The acting human's identity (available since every person got their own Matrix identity in the shared room) now travels with an interactive chat turn into credential resolution, so a commit, pull request or push is attributed to — and, when a personal credential is set, signed with — the person who actually asked for it. The **SSH-transport equivalent was also built for the first time**: an attributed push over SSH now prefers the acting human's own key, the same way an HTTPS push already preferred their personal token. Two things are unaffected by design: a scheduled or GitHub-triggered run still uses the team's static owner (there is no "acting human" for an autonomous run), and the claim is **bounded, not taken on trust** — the platform verifies the claimed sender is plausibly the real one for that specific agent (one of its own team's members, or its own owner for a standalone agent) before honouring it; an unverifiable sender is treated exactly like no sender at all, falling back to the pre-existing behaviour rather than risking a write under the wrong identity. (#2175)
- **Who can see a Working Environment or an MCP server is no longer something you pick on the form that creates or edits it** — both forms used to render an ownership/team picker (on the environment form it was already locked for anyone but a System Admin, but still shown to a Team Admin), even though visibility was always meant to follow the owning team automatically. The control is gone from both: an environment's or a server's reach is derived purely from its team's membership, never chosen by hand. A System Admin can still move an *existing* environment between team-owned, shared and global through **Administration → Resources**; there is no equivalent surface yet for an MCP server, so creating one with no team context now leaves it unassigned (reachable only to a System Admin, until published global or given a team by a future admin surface) rather than silently landing on the `default` team the way an omitted ownership used to. (#2195)
- **A scheduled or GitHub-triggered run now defers to the team's current admin, not the team's original creator — and a chat write with no personal credential set is refused, not silently rewritten under the team's shared identity** — an autonomous run's credential used to resolve to the static, immutable identity the agent was created under, which meant a Team Admin handover left every unattended run still attributed to whoever created the team long after they left. It now resolves to that team's **current** team admin instead, so a handover is honoured without redeploying the agent (a standalone agent, having no team to defer to, is unaffected). Separately, a **human chatting right now** whose account has no personal credential used to have their commit, PR or push silently attributed to the team's shared identity — the same courtesy an unattended run still gets, since it has no one to ask. That write is now **refused** with the existing "add a personal credential" message instead, because a person present in the conversation can be asked to add one rather than have their identity quietly swapped for the team's. Reads, clones and GitHub polling are unaffected by either change — they still fall back to a shared credential exactly as before. (#2176)
- **Creating a team no longer starts with a Working Environment you have to invent first** — building a team used to force a detour through a page that had nothing to do with the team itself: pick, or create, a Working Environment, then hand it to a team that did not exist yet. The **New Environment** button is gone from the Working Environments page, and every path that creates a team — the Agent Team Wizard and the Process-Canvas **Create team** dialog — now treats picking one as **optional**. Leave it unset and the team gets a fresh, repo-less environment of its own, the same one `POST /api/teams` has always auto-created when none is supplied; that is now the *only* way a new environment comes into being. A plain team member can go from nothing to a working team without ever naming or finding an environment first. Two small consequences follow from the removed button: the Working Environments page's empty state now points at team creation instead of a button that no longer exists, and the single-agent create form's "no environments yet" hint — which used to link to that button — now points at the Agent Team Wizard, since that is the only way one comes into being. Nothing about an *existing* environment changes: editing, deleting and the repository/credential/MCP/toolchain panels on the Working Environments page are untouched, and a repository-less environment behaves exactly as it always has. (#2168)

- **A model provider can now be published as global, so every user and agent can use it without belonging to a team** — until now a Model Provider was reachable only by the team that owned it (or, for a system-scoped one, the teams it was assigned to), which meant a brand-new user in no team yet — or a standalone agent with no team at all — had no model to run against until someone wired one up for them. A System Admin can now tick **Global provider** in the provider editor to lift that boundary: a global provider belongs to no team and is usable by **everyone**, and it is what lets a plain user's AI helpers bill against a model out of the box. The authority stays where the cost and governance are — only a System Admin sees the checkbox and only a System Admin can publish or retract a global provider, so a Team Admin may use one but never create one — while access opens to all: the provider shows a single **Global** pill in the table instead of a team assignment, the team picker disappears while the box is on, and a non-admin sees it with the familiar padlock (use it, don't manage it). Two limits are deliberate: a **subscription (OAuth)** provider can never be made global, because a subscription seat is single-user under Anthropic's Consumer Terms, so the checkbox is disabled for that auth type; and going global changes only who may *reach* the provider — the token is still never exposed and management is still System-Admin-only. (#2136)

- **The first administrator is now a real user account instead of a credential in a config file** — a fresh stack used to be administered by a login that was not a person: the console compared what you typed against `ADMIN_USERNAME`/`ADMIN_PASSWORD` from the deployment's configuration and let you in with no user record behind it. That worked, but it had no identity, and everything keyed on one quietly did not: a team you created recorded no creator, your own display preferences would not save, you could not own a personal SSH key or GitHub token, and the audit trail could only name you as the bare string `admin`. Those same values now **seed** an account rather than being one. At first boot — and only on a stack with no users at all — the platform consumes them once to create a genuine System-Admin **user record**, then never reads them again. That record appears in the **Users** list like everybody else, with its display name, e-mail, System-Admin flag and login password all editable there, and your session finally resolves to a real person. Four things follow, and all of them are worth knowing before you upgrade. **The password now lives on the record**, so editing `.env` and restarting no longer changes or revokes it — the Users page does; if you never set a strong one, the shipped `admin`/`admin` remains a real account, so change it there. **The configuration credential is a one-time seed window, not a standing login**: it authenticates *only* while the stack has no user record yet — enough to sign in to a fresh install and let the seed run — and the moment that first System-Admin record exists it is retired permanently and never authenticates again, not even as a break-glass. Once seeded, while the orchestrator is unreachable nobody can sign in, and a sign-in attempted during that window says so honestly ("the user store is unavailable ... try again in a moment") instead of claiming your password is wrong. Recovering a lost administrator is consequently a **host-level** action on the orchestrator's user store, which is where comparable systems put it — not a back door in the console. **The last System Admin can no longer be deleted or demoted**: the refusal is a server-side rule, and the Users page shows it in advance on a greyed-out Delete and a locked System-Admin checkbox rather than letting you find out afterwards. And on an SSO stack, `BOOTSTRAP_SYSTEM_ADMIN_SUBJECT` seeds that first administrator by identity with no password at all — it was documented long ago but never reached a shipped configuration, so until now it had never actually run. (#2059)

- **A board that shows where every ticket actually is** — the platform could work out each piece of work's real state from GitHub rather than from its labels, but there was nowhere to look at it: answering "where is everything?" still meant opening tickets by hand. There is now a **Flow Control** page, one per agent team, next to Flows in the sidebar. Each column is a stage of the lifecycle — waiting for a person, ready, being written, in review, needing rework, ready to merge, done — and each card is one piece of work, showing its title, the **agent that owns it**, **how long it has been sitting in that stage**, and, when something is holding it up, a **named blocker** you can hover for the specifics. Links on every card open the issue and the pull request directly. The important property is what the board *cannot* do: it never reads a ticket's labels, so a piece of work whose labels claim it finished, while its pull request is open and conflicting, appears in **review with a "claimed done" badge** — not in the Done column. That exact case is what hid the original stall for hours. Above the columns, one line per epic totals it up — *"Epic #2112: 3 done · 1 blocked · 2 in progress · 4 waiting"* — with the most-blocked epic first. Empty columns stay visible on purpose: where work is *not* is as useful to see as where it is. Two things it deliberately does not do. It **changes nothing** — there is no repair button anywhere on it, because the data behind it only observes; acting on what you see is still a person's job, which is what the deep links are for. And it is **honest about what it could not read**: maintenance mode, a team with no repository bound, and a partial GitHub outage each say so in place of showing a confident but stale picture. (#2116)

- **The platform can now tell you what state every ticket is really in, from GitHub rather than from its labels** — when an epic stalled, the reason it stayed hidden for hours was that the labels said everything was fine. A merger had reported "done" on a pull request that was open and conflicting; four more sat stranded for reasons nobody had a name for. Tracing them meant opening each one by hand. There is now a per-team **ticket ledger**: one entry per open piece of work, carrying where it has actually got to — waiting for a person, being written, in review, needing rework, ready to merge — and, when something is holding it up, **a named reason**. The states are worked out from the team's own flow, not from a fixed list of label names, so a team that calls its stages something else gets the same answer. Six blockers are worth calling out. **"claimed done, not merged"** is the one that catches a lying label: it fires when a stage reported itself finished, produced none of the signals it is supposed to produce, and the pull request is still sitting there — which is exactly the case that hid the original stall, while a perfectly healthy hand-off (a developer finishing and a reviewer picking the work up) is *not* flagged. **"superseded"** catches a duplicate: every file the pull request adds already exists on the main branch, so the work landed some other way. **"stale-base"** separates two things that look identical on the surface — a pull request whose checks are red because of its own bug (that is a code fix) from one that was fine until the main branch moved underneath it (that is a rebase). The others are **conflict**, **red checks**, **approved but nobody merged it**, plus the stalled and unclaimed cases the existing stall detection already found, now folded into one list instead of two. Each entry says whether a person is needed or an automated retry would do, and how long it has been stuck in its current state. Two things it deliberately does **not** do. It **changes nothing** — this release only observes, so nothing is closed, relabelled or retried on your behalf. And it **declines rather than guesses**: if GitHub has not finished working out whether a pull request merges, or the file listing came back truncated, or a repository could not be read at all, the affected checks stay silent and say what could not be read instead of inventing a finding. A partial GitHub outage makes the ledger less complete, never wrong. There is no screen for it yet — the board and the central inbox that read this data are the next steps. (#2115)

- **A flow can now declare how many tickets it works at once, and the platform holds the rest** — an epic dispatched across an agent team stalled, and the cause was upstream of everything anyone was looking at: the dispatcher fed several tickets in at once, their pull requests raced one shared `main`, and the resulting merge conflicts were manufactured **by construction**. No amount of downstream detection un-makes a conflict that was created on purpose. The workaround was to tell the dispatching agent, in its prompt, to work one ticket at a time — advisory, per team, and re-derivable by a model on a bad day. A flow can now carry a **`wipLimit`** in its bundle, and the platform enforces it in code at the moment a dispatch would fire. With `wipLimit: 1` — the right setting for a team whose agents share one branch — the second ticket is **held** until the first is genuinely finished, and the count of what is in flight is computed by the platform from GitHub itself (which issues still carry an active flow label, which pull requests are still open) rather than from anything an agent says about its own progress. Four things are worth knowing. A **ticket**, not a run, occupies the lane: one ticket legitimately wakes several agents in turn — the developer's own pull request waking the reviewer is the *same* piece of work moving forward — and those hand-offs always go through, so a limit of one does not deadlock the flow on its own first step. A held dispatch is **never dropped**: it waits in place and fires the moment capacity frees, so a limit can delay work but cannot lose it. A **stuck** ticket keeps holding the lane rather than letting a second one route around it — a conflicting pull request, a red check or a ticket parked on a human gate is exactly the situation where starting something else makes the pile-up worse. And when GitHub cannot be read at all, nothing is released: an outage must not quietly open the gate. Setting a limit is **opt-in**; a flow that declares none dispatches exactly as it does today, and costs nothing extra to run. (#2114)
- **Share… — a team's member list now lives in the Agent Chat, not in an admin page** — until now the only way to give a colleague access to a team was the **Access** page's members list, which is one page away from the work and framed around a concept ("access scopes") that has nothing to do with the team you are looking at. The team's own context menu in the Agent Chat now carries **Share…**, and it is the whole surface: who may open this team and chat with its agents, and which of them administers it. A **Team Admin** adds an existing colleague, switches somebody between **Team Admin** and **Team Member**, and removes people — no System Admin, no second page. A **Team Member** sees the **same list, read-only**: no role picker, no Add row, no Remove button, and the panel says why. That is deliberate and it is the one entry in that menu that is *not* greyed out without rights, because seeing who is on your own team is something a member is allowed to do; changing it is not. The trimming is a courtesy rather than the protection — every write is checked again on the server against the same rule, so a hand-made request from a Team Member is refused whatever the buttons look like. **The picker searches everyone in the organisation.** People are findable by name and email — the private thing is the team, not the person — and it shows a display name and an email address and **nothing else**: never whether somebody is a System Admin, never their GitHub handle. A System Admin therefore turns up in the results as an ordinary colleague, which is the intent. Only people who **already have an account** are offered: there is no invite-by-email here, and a colleague who has never signed in still has to be created once on the **Users** page by a System Admin — once per person, not once per team, and this release does not change that. **The last Team Admin cannot simply leave.** Removing them — or demoting them to Team Member — would leave a team nobody can administer, so the panel asks who takes over, offers only people **already on the team**, and does both in a single operation: the successor is promoted in the same write that removes or demotes the outgoing admin, so there is no moment in between and no half-applied state if something fails. When the last admin is also the team's only member there is nobody to hand over to, and the panel says so — add somebody first, or delete the team — rather than showing an empty picker. **Removing somebody removes their access, not their account**: their user record, their personal credentials and everything they did are untouched; they simply stop seeing the team. One deliberate deviation from the original specification, stated because it is visible: the design asked for the **Share…** entry to be *greyed out* for a Team Member, which would have made the read-only list unreachable — and a later phase retires the Access page's members list as superseded by this panel, which would have left a member with no way at all to see who they are working with. The entry is therefore live for anyone who can see the team, and the hint the design asked for is delivered on the entry itself. (#2082)

- **The whole team-sharing loop is now proven end to end, by two people at once** — every automated browser journey the platform ships has, until now, run as a single operator who could see everything. That is a comfortable way to test a feature and a useless way to test *access*: the most important thing the sharing redesign promises is that a colleague **cannot** see your team until you share it, and an assertion that nothing is hidden from an all-seeing administrator can never fail. A new journey drives the real product as **two separate signed-in people**, each in their own browser, through the entire story: Ada creates a team; Bea does not see it; Ada shares it from the team's own **Share…** dialog as a Team Member; Bea can now open it but change nothing; Ada tries to step down while she is the only administrator and is refused, and asked who takes over; Ada promotes Bea; Bea can now manage the list; Ada hands the team over and leaves. Two things make it worth more than a checkbox. **Every “cannot” is checked twice** — once that the control is absent from the screen, and once that the server refuses the request when it is sent directly with that person's own session, because a hidden button is a courtesy and the refusal is the actual boundary. And the journey ends by proving the rule that only matters on a bad day: a team can be handed from its creator to a colleague and back without ever, at any instant, being left with nobody who can administer it. The supporting change is that the disposable test stack now runs in **multi-user mode**, which is what makes any of these predicates capable of failing; the shipped product's own default is unchanged, and no other journey behaves differently, because the administrator those journeys sign in as sees the same thing in either mode. (#2086)

- **The separate Access page is gone — its System-Admin duties moved into one Administration surface** — now that a team's members are managed from its own **Share…** menu, the central **Access** page had nothing left that a team's own admins could not do themselves, so it was **dissolved rather than renamed**. The old **Users** page becomes **Administration**, a single System-Admin-only entry with three tabs. **Users** is the human-account list you already had. **All Teams** lists every Agent Team on the platform — its owner, its member agents, the people on it, who administers it, whether it is enabled, and a **no admin** marker on any team nobody can currently administer — and lets a System Admin delete one; it deliberately manages no memberships, because that is the Share dialog's job now. **Resources** shows the ownership tier of each agent and Working Environment and edits it through the same ownership control the credential forms use — which also **closes a bug**: the old flat team selector, used on a **shared** Working Environment, silently un-shared it from every other team that was using it, and the new control states the resulting tier before you save. Navigating to the old `/teams` address now lands on Administration, so an existing bookmark still resolves rather than dead-ending. **One capability is deliberately removed with the page**: an access scope can no longer be renamed or deleted from the console (minting a new one was already gone), because an older team layer that still backs it is retired wholesale by a later change; the underlying API verbs are untouched, so the surface can be restored with no server change if this is missed. (#2085)

- **Anyone can now create their own team, and it is private until they share it** — creating a team used to be an administrator's job twice over: the *access scope* it had to live in was System-Admin-only, and the team-creation screen itself demanded a repository, an SSH key and a GitHub token before it would do anything. A new colleague with an account and nothing else could not get started without two other people. Now **any signed-in person can create an Agent Team**, and they become its **Team Admin** in the same action — the team, its agents, its chats, its workspaces and the Working Environment created alongside it are visible to them and to nobody else. A second ordinary user does not see it exists; a System Admin still can, for oversight, exactly as before. **A team can also now be created with no infrastructure at all** — no repository, no key, no token, no model provider — which produces the same repository-less team the platform already supported when reusing a shared environment; supply a repository and every existing check applies unchanged. **A team can never be left without an administrator.** Removing or demoting the *last* Team Admin of a team is refused unless you name a successor who is already a member of it, and deleting a *user* is refused while they are the last Team Admin of anything — the refusal names every team that blocks it and both ways past it (promote somebody, or delete the team). Naming a successor promotes them and steps you down in one operation, so the team is never briefly admin-less or briefly double-admin'd. That check runs *before* any of the offboarding work, so a refused deletion neither pauses the person's agents nor deletes their personal credentials. **One security hole closed on the way**, and it only becomes a hole once teams are private: adding roles to an **existing** team through the team-creation API was not permission-checked at all, so any signed-in user could have grown somebody else's team. It now requires Team Admin rights over that team, on the same rule every other team change already used. **One capability is deliberately removed**: the **New Team** button on the Access page is gone and the API behind it now answers *410 Gone* naming its successor, because an Agent Team *is* the team now — the access scopes that already exist stay fully manageable (rename, membership, delete), there is simply no way to mint a new one. The practical consequence, stated rather than hidden: a new *team-scoped credential* must name one of the existing scopes, or be personal or system-scoped. Sharing a team with a colleague is still done through the members list on the Access page; a dedicated Share dialog with a successor picker is the next step and is not in this release. (#2081)

- **A flow label that says "completed" now means the work actually landed** — the platform swaps an agent's claim label from `<label>:wip` to `<label>:completed` when its run finishes, and that was all it checked: the *run* completing, not the run *achieving* anything. A merger that hit a merge conflict, reported it and exited cleanly still got `ready-to-merge:completed` stamped on an open, conflicting pull request. Every surface that reads those labels — the flow graph, the anomaly detector, the hill-climb metrics — was then confidently wrong, and a human had to trace each stalled ticket by hand to find out which "completed" labels were lying. A label can now declare an **observable post-condition**, and the forward swap only happens once it actually holds on GitHub: a merge label requires the linked pull request to be **merged** *and* its issue **closed**; a review label requires a **submitted review**; a PR-producing label requires a **pull request to exist**. When the run finishes but the condition does not hold, the claim deliberately stays `:wip` — which is exactly the state the existing stall detector already surfaces once the agent stops running — and the reason ("pull request #2105 is not merged") is recorded on the run. Verification also fails **closed**: if GitHub cannot be read, or the declared condition is one the runtime does not recognise, the claim stays `:wip` rather than being waved through, because an unverifiable claim is not a completed one. A run blocked this way is now also measured as a failure rather than a success, so the same false "done" cannot reappear in the agent performance metrics. Declaring a post-condition is **opt-in and fully backwards-compatible**: a label without one behaves exactly as it did before and costs no extra GitHub call.

- **Working Environments and MCP servers can now be shared across teams, so a new team no longer needs its own infrastructure first** — both were owned by exactly one team, with no way to mark one as shared. That made every new team a setup project: before it could do anything it needed a repository, an SSH key, a GitHub token and a model provider of its own, because it could not see anybody else's. An environment or MCP server can now be created as **shared** and assigned to any number of teams; assigned teams see it in their pickers and use it exactly as if it were their own. Combined with the existing repository-less path, that means **a team can now be created with no infrastructure at all** — an operator provisions one shared environment once, and every team afterwards just selects it. Who may do what follows the split you would expect: **any** Team Admin of an assigned team can edit a shared record's content, because an environment is configuration rather than a secret and needing an operator for every branch change would defeat the point; deciding **which** teams a record is shared with, or moving one between shared and team-owned, stays with a System Admin. Both the Working Environments and the MCP Servers form gained the same ownership control the SSH-key and GitHub-token forms already had — neither page previously offered any way to say who owns the thing you were creating. Existing environments and MCP servers are untouched and behave exactly as before: nothing is re-assigned, nothing changes hands, and a record with no explicit setting is still owned by its team. **One change narrows an existing permission and is worth reading twice**: registering, editing and deleting an MCP *server* had no permission check at all — any signed-in user could do it, unlike the MCP credentials beside them — and now requires Team Admin rights over the server's owning team. Two ungated reads closed with it: an environment's full record (its repositories and its GitHub token reference) and an MCP server's raw definition were both readable by any signed-in user who knew the id. One deliberate omission: this release adds the shared tier but does **not** re-point credentials, model providers and skills onto the new team concept — that turned out to depend on retiring an older team layer that still drives the Access page and five dropdowns, so it is left whole for the change that can do it properly rather than delivered as a third of itself. (#2083)

- **Every human now gets their own Matrix account, so the chat model can stop being shared** — until now the platform had exactly **one** human Matrix identity: the shared admin whose token lives in `MATRIX_ACCESS_TOKEN`. Every agent chat room was a 1:1 between an agent and *that* one account, which meant one admin token could read every person's conversation with every agent — named in the governance concept as the live privacy risk, with per-user Matrix identity as the real fix. This is the first half of that fix, and it is deliberately only the first half. **Creating a human on the Users page now also provisions that person's own Matrix account**, named `@u-<hash>:local` — derived from their login identity rather than from the record's internal id, so somebody who is offboarded and later re-onboarded comes back to the *same* account instead of a fresh empty one, and normalised the same way the user store compares a login, so two spellings of one identity can never end up as two accounts. The hash is deliberate: a login is usually an email, which is not legal in a Matrix name and would be visible to everyone in every shared room — the **display name**, set from the person's real name, is what colleagues actually read. That shape is now **reserved platform-wide**: agent names are free-form slugs, so without this an agent could be created that happens to occupy a person's Matrix account and inherit their rooms; the check sits at the single point every Matrix account in the stack is created through, and is narrow enough that an ordinary name like `u-payments` is still perfectly legal. The person's Matrix access token is stored **server-side only, in its own file separate from the user record** — the record carries only the account name — because the one secret the user record already holds is kept out of responses by a convention that every present and future code path has to remember, and a leaked login token is somebody being impersonated rather than a password that still has to be cracked. Deleting a human retires their Matrix account and deletes the stored token, alongside the personal SSH keys and GitHub tokens that offboarding already removed. Two honest limits, one of them since narrowed. **The room itself is still one per agent, shared by whoever talks to it** — a genuine per-person room model is a separate, still-unimplemented follow-up. Sending into it, though, no longer goes through the console's own admin: a later change (#2173) moved `POST /api/matrix/rooms/:roomId/messages` onto each human's own Matrix account (inviting it into the room on first use), so `event.sender` on a human's message is that person, not `@admin:local` — this release is what made that identity exist to send with. And **a single-operator stack is completely unaffected**, not by a setting but structurally: it creates no user records at all, so it provisions no accounts. Provisioning is also best-effort by nature rather than by choice — the homeserver we run has no admin API, so account creation is a message to an admin room whose reply is never read, and the subsequent login is the only real success signal; a failure therefore leaves the person's record intact without an account, and the next attempt repairs it rather than a half-created state being left behind. The one identity that is pointedly *not* auto-provisioned is the very first administrator's, because that identity has to adopt the existing shared rooms and their history when the room model lands, and handing it an empty new account first would only get in the way. (#1458)

- **Multi-user mode can now be switched on without losing anything — and is off until you do** — the team scope model has been in the code for a while, but on a single-operator stack it never did anything: the one login is the operator, so no list was ever filtered. That made "is this stack enforcing team boundaries?" a question nobody could answer from the outside, and it left activation looking like a leap. There is now an explicit **`AUTH_ENABLED`** switch. Leave it unset — the default — and the console behaves exactly as it does today: whoever signs in is the operator, sees everything and can manage everything. Set it to `true` and the same console starts showing each person only what their user record's team memberships allow. **Nothing has to be migrated between those two states**, because the adoption already happened: on every restart the **Default Team** takes ownership of every agent, Working Environment, credential and — newly — every **MCP server** that predates the ownership model, so flipping the switch is a restart and not a data migration. Two gaps closed along the way. The bootstrap administrator is a *System Admin*, which is a global flag rather than a team role, so the Default Team that owns all of that had no **Team Admin** at all; it now gets one — once, and never re-asserted, so an admin you deliberately demote stays demoted. And MCP servers were being *filtered* by an owner they were never *given*, relying on a fallback inside the check rather than a value on disk. The switch deliberately governs authorization only: the login is unchanged in both modes, an unauthenticated request still sees nothing, personal credentials stay private to their owner in either mode, and the single-sign-on auto-login gate is untouched. The one sharp edge is called out rather than hidden — turning the switch back *off* on a stack that already has named users hands each of them full visibility, so the console says so in its log at startup, naming how many people are affected.

- **Flow drafts are now private to you, and shared on purpose** — every draft in the Flows page used to sit in one global pile: anyone signed in could open, rename, overwrite or delete anyone else's in-progress design, prompts and Copilot conversation included, and nothing said whose was whose. A draft now belongs to the person who created it and is visible only to them; the Drafts list marks each row **private** or **shared with N people** and names the creator of one you did not make. A System Admin still sees everything. **Share…** in the draft's row menu opens a panel listing who has access and a picker of colleagues to add — existing accounts only, shown by name and email and nothing else. Everyone you share with becomes a **full equal**: they can edit, rename, export, create a team from the draft, share it on, remove anyone — including you — and delete it. That is deliberate and it is the one thing to read twice before pressing Share: **sharing a draft is a one-way act of trust, not a lend.** If that is more than you mean, export the draft and send the file instead — an imported bundle is a separate draft belonging to whoever imported it and carries no access list. Two rules follow from the equality: the access list can never be emptied (the last person is told to delete the draft rather than leave it), and deleting a draft is confirmed with the other holders named. Nothing changes for existing drafts on upgrade — every one of them keeps exactly the reach it has today, so no re-sharing is needed. The Agent Team Wizard's draft gallery is filtered by the same rule, since it reads the same list. Deleting a user record drops them from every access list without deleting anyone's work.

- **The orchestrator's internal API can now be locked down** — the control plane the admin console talks to on port `4002` has never been published to the host, but it has always been reachable by everything on the container network, agent containers included. That meant an agent that called it directly could do anything the console can do: read and write any other agent's configuration, enable or delete agents, edit teams, users and memberships — every permission check the console performs was, in principle, bypassable. It is now behind an authentication gate, and the two kinds of caller get very different keys. The **admin console** holds one shared token and keeps the full surface, which is right: it has already checked what the signed-in person is allowed to do before it forwards anything. Every **agent container** instead receives a token of its own, valid only for the handful of routes an agent legitimately needs — pushing its own session log and token usage, reading its own configuration, editing its own triggers, recording knowledge, committing and opening pull requests, waking the next agent in a flow — and only ever for **its own** identity. An agent asking about another agent is refused. Nothing else can present a credential at all. Two things are worth knowing before you switch it on. First, **it is off until you set one value**: with no `INTERNAL_API_TOKEN` in your `.env` the platform behaves exactly as it did before, so upgrading changes nothing on its own. Second, when you do set it, it starts in **observe** mode — it allows every call but writes a log line for each one it *would* have rejected, so you can spend a week finding out who actually talks to the port (including the integration nobody remembers setting up) before anything breaks. `docs/deployment.md` walks through the three stages and the one `grep` that tells you when it is safe to enforce. Rotating the token is a value change plus a restart of the affected agents: each agent's key is derived from the shared one, so changing it invalidates every outstanding key at once and each agent is issued a fresh one the next time it starts. One deliberate omission: the design also described a third key class for external systems announcing Briefing Pack changes, and that endpoint no longer exists — the Briefing Pack model was removed earlier — so a key class nothing can use was not built.

- **A repository can now be attached to a single agent, outside its Working Environment** — repositories have always been an environment-level concept: the environment lists them, and every agent in it gets them. That is too blunt when only one agent needs one more — a reference codebase to read, a second service it has to touch, a documentation repo — and the only workaround was to widen the shared environment, which quietly changed the other agents' prompts and workspaces too. The **Workspace** view in Agent Chat now has a **repositories** button, and the agent's **Settings** tab carries the same panel next to *Sync repositories*: both list every repository the agent has and let you attach another one to **that agent alone**. Inherited rows are marked *From Working Environment* and offer no remove action — the environment stays the only place to change those — while an attachment is marked *This agent only*. The form is the environment's own repository card, field for field, including the live reachability check with its three distinguishable results, and like there the check is advice rather than a gate. The repository is cloned into the same `./<repo-name>/` path an environment repository would use, appears in the file tree without a manual refresh, is listed in that agent's generated context the same way (read-only annotation included), and is re-cloned after a container respawn or a workspace reset, so the agent never loses a repository its prompt still names. On a **repository-less** environment the attachment now also switches on the git / `gh-as-agent` working instructions, which used to be gated on the environment alone. Four boundaries are worth knowing. There is **no new credential**: the clone uses the existing SSH-key registry or the environment's GitHub token, and a repository neither can reach is refused *before* any clone is attempted, naming which credential was tried — in particular the "HTTPS URL, but this environment has no GitHub token" case. A **directory-name collision** (two `…/tools` repositories, or a plain folder already at that path) is likewise refused with the conflict named, and nothing is left half-cloned; there is no automatic renaming. Only a user who may **manage** the agent can attach or remove one — a chat-only user sees the list but no action, and a direct API call from one is refused. And **removing** an attachment deletes the working copy, with the confirmation naming what that costs by count: how many uncommitted changes and how many unpushed commits, or that the copy is clean. Attaching during a run is safe and allowed (it only adds a directory, and the agent is told about it from its next run on); removing during a run is refused, because those are files the run may be using. Nothing about the Working Environment data model changed, and a later *Sync all agents* neither propagates an attachment to siblings nor removes it from the agent that has it.

- **Every Agent Team and standalone agent now records who owns it — invisibly, and without changing anything you can see** — the platform had no idea which human owned an Agent Team. Access was decided by a separate "access scope" layer, and the field meant for an owner (`ownerId`) was read in five places and written in none. This lays the foundation the new access model needs: every Agent Team and every standalone agent gets an owner, each Agent Team gets its own member list with per-member roles (Team Admin / Team Member), and a one-off migration runs at startup so that **nobody loses access to anything they can reach today**. There is deliberately **no behaviour change** — existing lists, chats, routes and permissions return exactly what they returned before, which is asserted by a fixture test that runs the real permission predicates over every user against every resource and fails if a single one loses reach. Two things are worth knowing. An owner assigned by the migration is a **defensible placeholder, not a recovered fact**: nothing anywhere records who originally created a team, so the migration picks the scope's longest-standing Team Admin, records that the owner came from the migration rather than from a person, and it can be corrected — and because any Team Admin can act regardless, a wrong owner is a cosmetic error rather than a lockout. Second, an access scope with no members at all leaves its resources unowned and **says so in the startup log** rather than inventing an owner for them. Ownership also follows a *Convert into a team* conversion onto the new team, and a team member never carries an owner of its own — it follows its team.

- **A human message in the Agent Chat now names who wrote it, and "right" finally means "mine"** — every message a person sent was stamped with the same generic **YOU** badge and put on the right-hand side. In a setup where several people talk to the same agent that made the transcript unreadable as a record of a conversation: you could not tell your own message from a colleague's, and the right-hand side meant nothing more than "not the agent". Two things change. The badge now shows the **author's initials** — the first letters of the first two words of their display name, a single letter for a one-word name — with the full name revealed on hover **and** on keyboard focus. And the sides now carry meaning: **your own messages stay on the right, everyone else's move to the left**, next to the agent, while keeping the human bubble colour so they are never mistaken for the agent's. The badge and the side are deliberately derived from different things and are *not* unified: the badge answers *who wrote this* and never depends on who is reading, so the same message shows the same initials to everybody while sitting on opposite sides for its author and for a colleague. The name is read from the user record when the transcript is rendered rather than frozen at send time, so renaming somebody on the Users page updates their initials on messages they sent months ago. There is deliberately no per-person colour — the side and the initials carry the distinction, and two people whose initials collide are told apart by the hover name. **This was not only a labelling change**: the platform genuinely did not record *which* console user sent a chat message — every human message is delivered under one shared Matrix identity — so the author is now captured on the message itself, established server-side from your session so a browser cannot attribute its message to a different person, and applied equally to a typed message, a quick-reply answer clicked on your behalf and the pre-hand-off check. The one honest consequence: **messages that already exist can never be attributed retroactively.** They keep the right-hand side they have always had and get a neutral person icon instead of letters, because moving every message anyone ever sent to the left would gain no truth — so for that older content the right-hand side is not a guarantee of authorship. The same neutral icon appears for a message posted by some other Matrix client and for an author whose user record has since been deleted. Trigger notices, finished-loop cards and every other automated entry are untouched. The raw Matrix ids still shown in the trigger-notice footer and the session log are a separate surface and a separate follow-up. (#2065)

- **Agents can now hand off directly to a teammate, drawn and edited in the Flow Editor** — until now one agent woke another by setting a **label** that the other was listening for. That is the right mechanism when a person should be able to read the state off the issue or pull request, but it forced you to invent a label for pure coordination that has no natural home on a work item. A **direct hand-off** skips it: an outcome names its teammate and wakes it inside the platform, with no label, no issue and no pull request — the event log is the audit trail. The delivery itself shipped in #2030 but had to be hand-written into `triggers.json`; it is now a first-class part of the canvas. Drag from one agent to another and the chooser offers **Hand off directly** alongside the label and channel tiles; the hand-off draws as its own indigo edge carrying an **↪ \<Target\>** chip and no label token, so it is never mistaken for a label hand-off, and the legend lists it next to one. Clicking the edge opens a dedicated **Edit hand-off** dialog with just the two things a hand-off has — the target role and an optional note — and deliberately *without* a label field or a Delivery selector, because a direct hand-off is internal by construction and offering that toggle would imply a GitHub path that does not exist. The same hand-off is authorable from the Routing tab's action list, whose row was read-only in the previous release. The receiving agent's **wake instruction** is derived for you and appears in its Triggers tab as *Direct hand-off from …*; it is one per agent rather than one per incoming arrow, so several teammates handing off to the same agent all wake it the same way — and a wake instruction you wrote by hand is left alone. Two limits are enforced rather than documented: the target must be another agent on the same team (never a person — a hand-off is delivered to the target's queue and a human role has none), and never the producer itself. The note reaches the receiving agent as *context to check*, never as its instructions. The Flows help topic gains a **Label hand-off or direct hand-off?** chapter, because the honest difference between the three hand-off mechanisms is not speed or reliability — all of them use the same queue — but what a human can see afterwards. (#2031)

- **The + Create launcher now ranks the ways in, simplest first, and every entry says where it lands** — the launcher offered five equally-weighted tiles, which answered *what can I do here* but left *where should I begin* to the person with the least basis for deciding. It is now explicitly ranked, simplest to most advanced, in three tiers. **Chat with one assistant** is the hero: full width, first, badged *Fastest start* and carrying its own **Start chatting** button — the fastest thing you can do on this screen, and the right answer when you are not sure. Below it, one bordered group headed *Or build a team of agents that work together (a flow)* holds the three ways to build a team — **Start from a ready-made team** (badged *Recommended*), **Describe what you need** and **Design it on the canvas** — and that heading is the first place the product says what a flow actually is, and states that all three options end in the *same* flow and differ only in how you start it. The specialist path moves out of the way into a collapsed **Advanced options** disclosure and is renamed **Configure an agent manually**, after what it asks of you: it used to sit under the wordiest title on the page and read as the *simpler* of the two single-agent paths while actually being the expert one. Every entry now states what it opens as a small **→** line — *Opens a chat with your assistant*, *Review the team, then launch it*, *Opens an editable draft on the canvas*, *Opens the canvas*, *Opens the agent editor* — and the three flow cards additionally reveal a small hand-drawn sketch of that kind of screen: hover one, tab to it, or tap the eye control on a touch device (#2034 kept the sketch to that group, where the destination is genuinely non-obvious — the hero and the *Advanced* entry keep only the → line, since a chat window and a manual form need no drawing). They are deliberate schematics rather than screenshots, so they cannot drift out of date, and the caption beside each one carries the meaning. The badges are advice, not restriction, and an *Advanced* marker means "this one asks more of you", not "you may not": every entry is still one click away, and the disclosure starts collapsed for everyone on every visit rather than remembering that you once opened it. One promise is deliberately understated — *Design it on the canvas* says only *Opens the canvas*, because the guided first step on an empty canvas does not exist yet; what you get today is a centred prompt to add your first agent, never a blank page. **Nothing was rewired**: every entry hands off to exactly the route it did before, so this is a re-sort, a re-label and a preview — no form, no endpoint and no destination changed. (#2027)

- **Model Providers help now explains each part of the page, and the "?" next to each one opens the right chapter** — the Model Providers page carries the console's two most consequential choices (which credential a provider authenticates with, and whether to probe it before saving) and all of it sat behind one **?** at the top, a whole document away from the controls it explains. The help is now a readable reference in six parts: what a Model Provider is (what each column shows, and the precedence chain that decides which model an agent actually runs on — agent override, then the Working Environment's default, then the built-in `.env` fallback); **API key / proxy authentication**; **Anthropic subscription authentication**, with the single-user Consumer-Terms rule as its own section; **Test connection and Discover models**; **Health**; and **changing credentials and deleting**. Five surfaces now carry their own small **?** that opens the help **at that chapter** instead of at the top — the page header, the authentication choice (which follows your selection, so it lands on whichever of the two auth chapters you are actually looking at), the subscription box, the Test/Discover row and the **Health** column — and a **?** pressed inside the provider form now opens the help *beside* the form rather than behind it. One thing that was never written down anywhere is now stated plainly, and it is worth reading before you register a provider without a token: leaving the token empty means the *probe* runs unauthenticated, but a running **agent** still inherits the platform's own `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN` from the stack configuration — a tokenless provider overrides only the Base URL, so the shared credential is sent to whatever endpoint you named. Only subscription auth clears those inherited variables. The help also now says why a probe always names the credential it used, the three states in which a probe is blocked outright rather than left as a dead button, and that a **Health** of `Unknown` is an honest never-measured status rather than a warning — health is a signal, not a gate, and nothing stops an agent using a provider that is currently red. Nothing about the page's behaviour changed, and every hint that sits next to a decision — the Base-URL note, the `claude setup-token` instructions, the probe's own credential line, the restart-agents dialog and above all the required single-user confirmation — stays exactly where it was. (#1988)

- **Plugins help now explains every surface on the page, and the "?" next to each one opens the right chapter** — the Plugins page carries a lot that was never spelled out (what a plugin actually is, why install and grant are two separate steps, the platform gate that greys a capability out, scope granularity and quota, what activating does versus what uninstalling leaves behind, plugins that bring their own UI, and per-team assignment) and all of it sat behind a single page-level **?**. The help is now a readable reference in eight parts — **What a plugin is**, **Install and grant are two different steps**, **Capabilities and the platform gate**, **Scope granularity and quota**, **What activating does — and what uninstalling undoes**, **Plugins with their own UI**, **Assigning a plugin to a team on this page**, and a **Deep dive: how a plugin works, and how to build one** — and five surfaces each carry their own small **?** that opens the help **at that chapter** instead of at the top: the page header, the Install and Uninstall confirmation dialogs, the team-assignment panel, and the footer of the installed list. Two corrections ride along. The Plugins page had ended up with **no page-level ? at all** — lost in the same #1847 header-unification change that had stripped it from the Skills page (#1990) — which is fixed. And the deep-dive on how a plugin works and how to build one, which disappeared when the standalone `/plugin-system` page was removed (E5, #1879), is back as the topic's final chapter rather than lost from the product. Three things the page could never state on its own are now written down: a platform mismatch shows an **install button that is enabled but warns** while the assignment gate quietly refuses, so "enabled" is not "will work here"; a button that is **absent entirely** means the plugin's manifest is invalid rather than that you lack permission; and **uninstalling revokes nothing** — the grants a delete leaves behind are deliberate, not a bug. Every hint that sits next to a control — the quota line, the capability cards, the *requests no host privileges* note and the point-of-decision copy in both confirm dialogs — stays exactly where it was. (#1986)

- **Working Environments help now explains each part of the form, and the "?" next to it opens the right chapter** — one correction first, because it changes what you should do: the **Description** field said it was *"shown only in this console — not passed to the agent"*, and that is the opposite of what happens. An environment's name **and** description are written into every agent's `CLAUDE.md`, right above its repository list, so a private note left there is read by the agent. The field now says so. The rest of this is help: everything about this page sat behind a single **?** at the top, on a page whose actual decisions — the repository rows, the token, the toolchain registry — are all made *inside* a side panel, a whole document away from that button. The help is now a readable reference in seven parts: what a Working Environment is (including a table of **what an edit reaches and what it does not**, which is the page's most surprising behaviour); **repositories** (cloned when the agent is created, what the branch field does, read-only repositories, repo-less environments, and **bringing existing agents up to date**); **credentials** (an SSH URL needs a key on its own repository row, an HTTPS URL needs the environment's token, and repointing the token restarts the environment's running agents); **MCP server references**; **model provider binding** (agent override → environment default → platform fallback); **additional context**; and **toolchains**. The repositories area, the GitHub token and the Toolchains panel each carry their own small **?** that opens the help **at that part** instead of at the top, and a **?** pressed inside a side panel now opens the help *beside* that panel rather than behind it. Several things that were never written down anywhere are now stated plainly: the repository URL check is **advice, not a gate** — an environment whose repositories are all unreachable still saves; **Read-only** is an instruction in the agent's context, not a file permission, so the files are still writable on disk; an HTTPS URL with no token **refuses the clone** with a message rather than silently trying the SSH key you selected; saving an environment whose repository list changed now **offers** to bring the existing agents up to date — it clones an added repository and adjusts a changed one (branch, address or SSH key) in place, but **only where that agent's clone is clean and fully pushed**, never stashing, resetting, deleting or force-checking-out anything; declining leaves a *"not up to date"* badge on the list that runs the same catch-up, and a **removed** repository is never deleted from any agent; the propagation reaches paused agents too but skips a member that has not been materialized yet; **additional instructions** reach an agent that already exists only the next time that agent's prompt, skills or plugins are saved; and this form has **no MCP picker at all**, so an environment's MCP pairs come from the Agent Team Wizard or the API — saving the form never disturbs them. A delete refusal names only the first agent using the environment, and can be caused by an agent that is not in your list. Nothing about how environments behave changed, and every hint that sits next to a control — the SSH-key and read-only notes on a repository card, the URL probe's messages, the token and provider explanations, the CLAUDE.md field's note, the toolchain restart warning and the registry's image/platform hints — stays exactly where it was. (#1987)

- **The six smaller pages get a proper reference, and a "?" where a page really has a second surface** — About, Users, Access, Maintenance Mode, the Settings dialog and Agent Templates each had correct but thin help behind a single **?** at the top. All six are now readable references, and a deep-linked **?** was added only where a page genuinely has a second thing to explain. In the **Settings** dialog the existing **?** now follows you: on **Behavior** it opens the send-key chapter, on **Model** the assist-model chapter, on **Display** the theme and font-size chapter — and that chapter finally spells out what the storage table only implied, that your theme and Enter-key preference belong to the *browser*, not to you, so on a shared machine the next person inherits whatever you last chose, while the chat font size follows you anywhere you sign in. **Maintenance Mode** now has help for the state it is actually in: pressing **?** on the activate form explains what the grace period does and does not do, and — new — the card shown while the mode is *on* has its own **?** for what is currently being held back. Two things it used to claim are corrected: **Reset Session and Clear History are not blocked** during maintenance (only sending messages and the quick-reply buttons are), and the countdown never reaches the agents at all, which is why it can never end the mode by itself. The active-session counter is now honest about being a recent reading rather than a live probe, and about disabled or offline agents never counting. **Users** explains why the Subject cannot be changed (it is what every sign-in is matched against, so changing it would sever a person from their own account), that records come first for single sign-on too, and — with its own **?** right on the delete confirmation — exactly what an offboarding does: agents paused rather than deleted, personal credentials removed, memberships dropped, and the guard that does **not** exist, since nothing stops you deleting the last System Admin. **Access** gains a **?** inside the Manage panel covering what removing someone does and does not do, plus the personal-credential carve-out that no administrator can override and the two reasons a scope refuses to be deleted. **Agent Templates** gains a **?** on its two most opaque fields: *Invocation mode* (the two options describe one stored behaviour, and picking per-invocation also marks the agent autonomous — though any agent with a trigger is autonomous anyway) and the *Advanced* JSON box. It also stops over-promising: editing a built-in stores a **full snapshot**, so later platform improvements to that template are permanently shadowed until you **Reset**, and the role type is descriptive only — nothing filters on it, despite what the `pattern` option label says. **About** explains what the version hash means, why a name can be missing from the contributors list, and what to include in a problem report. Pressing **?** inside a side panel or the delete confirmation now opens the help *beside* it rather than behind it. Nothing about how any of these pages behaves changed, and every hint that sits next to a control — the role legend, the invocation-mode and role-type option labels, the built-in-override warning, the grace-period note and the password hints — stays exactly where it was. (#1996)

- **Create & Quickstart help now explains each way in, and the "?" next to each one opens the right chapter** — the **+ Create** launcher and the single-assistant page are the first two screens most people meet, and both shared one help topic behind one **?** at the top. The help is now a readable reference in four parts: **the entry paths** (what each of the five tiles produces — and the distinction that matters most on that screen, that two of them leave you a *draft* which is a plan rather than a running system, so nothing exists and nothing costs anything until you materialize it); **a single agent is a team of one**, with the consequence the one-line note on the page has no room for — **Grow into a full team creates a new team** rather than converting your assistant, and nothing moves across on its own, so knowledge and skills you want the team to have must be carried over deliberately; **the repository fast lane** (what the SSH probe actually buys you — it proves the key has access *and* reads the repository's real default branch, so a repository on `master` or `develop` is picked up without being asked — plus when an existing Working Environment and an existing agent are reused rather than duplicated, and why the **Name** and **Model Provider** fields are not used at all once you paste a repository URL); and **what you need before you start**. Each surface now carries its own small **?** that opens the help **at that chapter** instead of at the top: the launcher header, the one-assistant page header, the *team of one* note at the foot of that page, and the checklist under the create button. That last chapter answers a question the page could never answer on its own — why a red warning card and the checklist sometimes disagree: a card reports that the platform has no provider, SSH key or token *at all*, while the checklist lists only what blocks the form as you have filled it in, so on the repository path a missing provider shows as a card without blocking the button, and on the repo-less path an empty name blocks the button with no card. Nothing about what gets created changed, and every hint that sits next to a decision — the three prerequisite warning cards with their direct fix-links, the checklist itself and the one-line tile descriptions — stays exactly where it was. (#1992)

- **Knowledge help now explains each part of the page, and the "?" next to it opens the right chapter** — the Knowledge page is the console's densest concept screen (how agents learn, the autonomy ladder, review and the promotion gates, domains/consolidate/distill, and where the knowledge is actually stored) and all of it sat behind one **?** at the top — on a page that, after an earlier header change, had ended up with **no page-level ? at all**. The help is now a readable reference in six parts: Memory vs Knowledge vs Skills (and what the Flow and Table views each give you); how learning works in three steps (the three kinds, what *converged* means, and that every lesson keeps the agent, session and pull request it came from); the per-agent **digest** and what stays searchable; **Autonomy, Review and the promotion gates**; the **power tools** — domains, consolidate and distill — as the sequence they usually are rather than three unrelated buttons; and **configuration** — forgetting, assist model and storage. The page header, the *Team knowledge* column, the Domains and Consolidation panels, the skill inspector and the configuration panel each carry their own small **?** that opens the help **at that chapter** instead of at the top, and a **?** pressed inside the sliding configuration panel or the skill inspector now opens the help *beside* that surface rather than behind it. Three things that were never written down anywhere are now stated plainly. The **learning settings are not admin-only**: anyone who can see a team on this page can change its autonomy level, review mode, gates, forgetting horizon, assist model and storage location — worth knowing before you treat the ladder as an administrative control. The **assist model** setting exists because the curator and skill distillation run with nobody driving them, so they cannot use the per-user model picker in the header (and seats that sign in with an OAuth token are never offered, because that path is not billable). And **storage**: a separate knowledge repository is checked for reachability *before* the change is saved, a protected branch is flagged because pushes to it may be rejected, and moving the location never moves what is already stored — the team starts fresh at the new one. The digest chapter now says how the selection is actually made (all conventions first, then converged entries, then the most recent, until a fixed budget is full — everything past it is still reachable by search, so falling outside the digest loses nothing) and which entries never appear at all. Nothing about how the page behaves changed, and every short hint that sits next to a control — the three kind explanations, the domains and consolidation primers, and the Forgetting / Assist model / Storage field notes — stays exactly where it was. (#1985)

- **Credentials help: one explanation, and each of the three pages now opens it at its own part** — SSH Keys, GitHub Tokens and My Credentials share a single help topic, which is the right call (the "why you need both" story should be told once) but had an awkward side effect: whichever page you pressed **?** on, you landed at the top of an explanation that opens with SSH. From the GitHub Tokens page that meant scrolling past a third of a document to reach your own subject. The help is now a readable reference in six clearly-headed parts — why both an SSH key and a GitHub token, SSH keys, GitHub tokens, My Credentials, the ownership tiers and privacy, and rotation and runtime resolution — and each page's **?** opens it **at that page's chapter**. The three shared chapters deliberately keep no button of their own: the panel shows the whole topic and simply scrolls to where you asked, so they are one scroll away rather than hidden behind another control. Each chapter now describes what its page actually does, which several parts of the old text did not: generating a keypair versus pasting one and the public key you get to see exactly once, the full classic and fine-grained scope checklists, what the **Used by** column and the delete dialog are telling you, and the rename/rotate/delete actions on your personal credentials. Three points are corrected while we were in there — the delete dialog's "still in use" list is advice, not a lock (the button stays active and the server is what refuses, so a stale page can never let a still-referenced key through), a Team Admin never gets edit or delete on a *system*-scoped credential, and a system credential assigned to no team stays visible to System Admins only. Every warning that sits next to a control stays exactly where it was: the "GitHub will not show this again" notices, the scope checklists, every **Configure SSO** step, and the rotate panel's warning that restarting your agents cuts off work in flight. If you sign in as the environment administrator and cannot own personal credentials at all, the **?** on My Credentials still works and now explains why. (#1993)

- **Token Usage help now explains each tab and the attribution model, and every "?" opens the right chapter** — the page carries two quite different views and a subtle rule about which piece of work a run's tokens are credited to, and all of it sat behind a single page-level **?**. The help is now a readable reference in four parts: what the page shows (only **agent runs** produce records — the console's own AI helpers never appear here — that the page is System-Admin-only rather than a filtered view for everyone else, why nothing is ever priced, what the four token classes mean and what *All time* actually reaches back to); the **Statistics** tab (the totals, what *Invocations* counts, and the two things that make a grouped table look like it does not add up — grouping by model fans a run across its per-model split, and a record with no team, provider or model lands in an explicit `unknown` bucket); the **Work items** tab (how issues and pull requests are chained by `closes #N`, why the Ø-per-work-item averages deliberately exclude the unattributed remainder, and why a low coverage figure is usually not a fault); and how runs are attributed (the four signals **flow · created · event · branch** with the strongest one winning outright, what the **shared** and **flow-mismatch** flags mean, and that a record is filed under the day the run really happened rather than the day it arrived — so a backlog drained late does not show up as a spike). The page header, each of the two tabs and both attribution columns now carry their own small **?** that opens the help **at that chapter** instead of at the top. Asking what the other tab shows does not switch you to it. Every short hint that sits next to a figure — the tokens-not-costs line, the per-tile explainers and the four signal names — stays exactly where it was. (#1994)

- **GitHub Event Log help now explains the table, the chips and the queue — and the "?" next to each one opens the right chapter** — the whole explanation of this page sat behind a single **?** at the top, and it only covered what the page *is*: the columns of the Processed-events table, why some repositories never produce a row at all, and why the Pending queue stops draining were documented nowhere. The help is now a readable reference in four parts — what the log is (purely observational, visible to every signed-in user, kept for 7 days and written best-effort), the **Processed events** table with every column explained and where its rows come from, the **Outcomes** chips with a "should you worry?" note for each plus a step-by-step *my agent never woke up* path, and the **Pending queue** with the four reasons a backlog builds and what **Cancel** does — and the table toolbar, the outcome legend and the Pending-queue tab each carry their own small **?** that opens the help **at that chapter** instead of at the top. Two things that used to catch people out are now written down: a repository is polled only if a Working Environment holds a GitHub token for it *and* an enabled agent has an enabled GitHub-event trigger on it, so an unwatched repository shows **no rows at all** — not even *nobody's listening* — which means silence on this page is not by itself evidence that nothing happened; and queued entries have **no expiry**, so a disabled agent's backlog waits indefinitely and drains in order when it comes back. The honest caveat about the *digested* chip being currently unreachable is kept. Nothing about the log's behaviour changed, and every hint that sits next to a live control — the per-chip tooltips, the collapsible *"What do these outcomes mean?"* legend, the kind badges and both teaching empty states — stays exactly where it was. (#1995)

- **Agent Chat: add a member to a live team without building it yet** — adding a member to a running team always did the whole job immediately: chat account, workspace, repository clone, container. When you are assembling a team over several sittings that is the wrong default about half the time, and the chat's workaround for it was to create the member and then **disable** it — which is not the same thing at all: the clone had already happened and the container had already run, so you were left with a *paused* agent that merely looks like a not-yet-built one in the list. **Add member** now asks first. The dialog carries a **Materialize now** checkbox, ticked by default — a member you are adding on purpose is usually one you want working right away, so pressing straight through behaves exactly as before. Untick it and the member is created as a real **stub**: its configuration and triggers are written, nothing is cloned and nothing runs. It appears in that team's **Not materialized yet** section, where **Materialize** finishes it into an agent indistinguishable from one you had built at the start — the prompt, the label suffix and every substituted variable are resolved identically in both modes, so deferring costs you nothing later. The button tells you which way it will go (*Add member* vs *Add member (stub)*), and the confirmation afterwards reports what the server actually did rather than what was asked for. Existing API callers are unaffected: a request that says nothing about materialization still materializes, and one that sends something that is not a plain true/false is rejected outright instead of being guessed at — a caller that believes it deferred must never have a repository cloned behind its back. The equivalent button on the Agents page is unchanged. (#1958)

- **Flow Editor: every toolbar button now says what it does** — most buttons in the Flow Editor explained nothing at all: you had to guess at "Materialize…", "Copilot", "Fit", "Flow ↓" or "Archived", and the handful that did have a hint used the slow grey browser tooltip, which looks nothing like the polished dark tooltip the rest of the console uses. Every button in the three toolbars — the **Import / Describe flow… / New flow** actions in the page header, the whole **flow header row** (the Draft/Live badge, the rename pencil, Add agent, Add human, Stages, Labels, Copilot, Materialize…, Anomalies, Improvements, Edit as draft, Export) and the **canvas controls** (Fit, Expand All, Collapse All, the flow-direction toggle, Archived, Stages) — now shows the same small dark tooltip with a short sentence explaining the action, instantly, on hover **and** on keyboard focus. Nothing is left on the old grey tooltip. Greyed-out buttons keep explaining **why** they are greyed out rather than what they would have done: the existing "accept or discard the Copilot proposal first" and "add at least one agent" reasons are unchanged, and **Export** — which had no hint at all — gained its own two. The three canvas toggles say which way the click will go, so **Archived** reads *Show archived…* or *Hide archived…* depending on its current state instead of leaving you to work it out. Screen-reader users lose nothing in the switch: the rename control, which shows the flow's name and used to rely on the browser tooltip for the word *rename*, now carries it in its accessible name, and the mode badge announces the consequence rather than just "Draft". Long tooltips wrap instead of running off the edge of the window, and none of them can be clipped by the canvas. (#1999)

- **Agent Chat: see and start a team's not-yet-materialized members** — a team can be created with its members left as **stubs**: configuration and triggers only, with no chat account, no workspace and no container, so a fifty-role process does not clone fifty repositories on day one. Until now Agent Chat could not show such a member at all, which meant a team created that way looked *empty* — the list quietly dropped every row it could not open a chat for. Each team group now ends with a collapsed **Not materialized yet (N)** section listing them; the count is readable without expanding, a team with no stubs shows no section, and a team whose members are *all* stubs is a normal-looking group rather than a blank one. A stub row is marked **Not initialised** — the same word the Agents page uses — carries its own muted status dot so it is never mistaken for a *paused* agent, and cannot be opened as a chat, because there is no chat to open. Its context menu offers **Materialize**, which does exactly what the Agents page's **Activate** button does: creates the Matrix user, seeds the workspace with the skills, prompt and MCP configuration, clones the repositories and starts the agent — the result is indistinguishable from a member materialized at team-create time. The row shows a spinner while that runs (a role with repositories takes a while) and moves up into the active members on success, with no page reload. A stub whose team is disabled gets an inert **Materialize** with *enable the team first*, and so does a caller who may not manage the agent — and if the server refuses anyway, the reason is shown rather than swallowed. The **show disabled agents** toggle is untouched by all of this: *stub* and *paused* are different states and stay independently visible. Ordering, filters, group collapse, unread/busy indicators and drag-reorder are unchanged, and every other consumer of the agent list gets exactly the response it got before. (#1957)

- **Skills help now explains every verb on the page, and the "?" next to each one opens the right chapter** — the Skills page has a lot of verbs behind it (grant, activate, *Install into agent*, promote, move, adopt, distill, merge, import, export) and telling them apart was exactly what tripped people up, while the whole explanation sat behind one page-level **?**. The help is now a readable reference in six parts — what a skill is (including what the Content / Versions / Lineage / Usage tabs show and what the *in sync* / *outdated* / *diverged* / *conflict* / *orphaned* drift badges mean), the three tiers and which one an agent actually gets, grant vs activation vs *Install into agent*, the full action table with who may perform each one, what an overwrite replaces and what merging does to provenance and grants, and Skills vs Knowledge vs Memory — and the tier filter, the skill action toolbar, the Adopt panel, the Install-into-agent panel, the Merge dialog and the import controls each carry their own small **?** that opens the help **at that chapter** instead of at the top. Two corrections ride along, both of which change what you should do: the help used to say a skill you *install* into a running agent is a one-off copy that the next workspace reset loses — it is not, because installing also writes the grant, so the choice between granting and installing is only about *when* you do it; and the Skills page had ended up with **no page-level ? at all** (it was lost in an earlier header change), which is fixed. A **?** that sits inside the skill detail panel or the Merge dialog now opens the help *beside* that surface rather than behind it. Every short hint next to a control — the "copy it to a team first" note, the Adopt-vs-Move-to-team blurb, the *Retire source skills* hint, the Distill primer and the three overwrite warnings — stays exactly where it was. (#1990)

- **Agent Chat: Import and New Agent moved into the page header** — every page in the console keeps its main actions in the top bar; Agent Chat was the exception, hiding create and import behind a small **+** in the agent list. Those two actions are now **Import Agent** and **New Agent** in the page header, styled and placed like everywhere else, and the **+** is gone — so neither is reachable from two different places. **New Agent** opens the **+ Create** launcher, which gained a fifth entry, **Create a single agent**: the direct "I know exactly what I want" form (id, role, Working Environment, skills, system prompt) that until now existed only on the Agents page. It is literally the same form — the two pages render one shared component, so they cannot drift, and nothing about what it creates has changed. **Import Agent** opens the ZIP picker in a single click and finishes through the import dialog you already know; the imported agent appears in the list without a reload, and if your list filter happens to hide it, the confirmation tells you so instead of leaving you looking at an unchanged list. If you are not allowed to create or import agents, both buttons are shown greyed out with the reason rather than silently missing. The agent list's own header row is unchanged (filter, show-disabled toggle, help), and its "no agents yet" message now points at the header button instead of offering a second, competing button of its own. (#1956)

- **Agent Chat help now covers the whole page, one click from wherever you are** — the in-app help for Agent Chat used to explain only the Triggers, Outcomes and Knowledge panels plus the session model, which meant most of what you see on screen went undocumented. It is now a complete, readable reference: what the page is, the **agent list** (grouping, reordering, the filter, the show-disabled opt-in, the busy/unread indicators, the row menu's Enable / Disable / Reset to stub / Delete / Mark as unread and the "+" create menu), the **chat header** (the learning badge, the model/provider selector and why switching provider restarts the agent, the context-usage indicator, *About this chat*, Reset Session vs Clear History), the **message composer** (the send keybinding, per-agent drafts, the slash-command palette, the unified Stop, the loop launcher, quick dictation vs the meeting note-taker, attachments and `@`-referencing a workspace file, clickable question cards), the **right rail** (Sessions, the session log and its live TODO panel, the Workspace browser and read-only vs read-write context folders) and the **Settings** tab — alongside the three panels it already covered. Each of those surfaces now carries its own small **?**, and clicking it opens the help panel **at that surface's chapter** rather than at the top of a long page; the row context menu, which cannot host a button, gets the same thing as a *What these actions do…* entry. A **?** whose control your agent does not have (no context folders, no team, no slash commands) opens the chapter's topic normally rather than showing anything empty or broken. Every short hint that sits next to a live control — the loop launcher's stop contract, the Outcomes empty state, the retire dialog's reversibility note, the Settings field hints — stays exactly where it was. (#1972)

- **Agent toolchain: pick a language stack per Working Environment** — an agent container runs one image, and the default carries only Python and Node — no JDK, Maven, .NET or Go — so an agent on a Java repository could edit the code but not build it. A Working Environment can now name a **toolchain**, and its agents are spawned from that toolchain's image; every other environment keeps the default, unchanged. Because an agent container holds the environment's SSH identity, GitHub token and model credential, the image is picked from a curated registry rather than typed in: only a **System Admin** registers a toolchain (the new **Toolchains** panel on the Working Environments page), and a Team Admin only *selects* an already-registered one on the environment form — a split enforced server-side, not by hiding a button. Changing an environment's toolchain restarts its running agents (an image is fixed for a container's lifetime), and the form warns you, naming how many are affected, before you do. A selection that is not registered, is not built for the runtime the stack uses, or whose image cannot be found makes the agent **refuse to start** with a message naming the environment and the toolchain, rather than quietly falling back to the default image and failing later at the first build command. This entry is the selection mechanism; the first toolchain image ships alongside it (see the next entry). (#1940)

- **A JVM toolchain you can actually build with — and a version your project owns** — the first shipped toolchain image lands, together with the registry and build path behind it. A System Admin now finds a **JVM** entry under **Available to install** in the Working Environments → Toolchains panel, reads a plain-language description of what it does, and installs it with one click — the image reference comes from the shipped manifest, so nobody retypes or mistypes it. Point a Working Environment at it and its agents can compile, run unit tests and run Maven. The important part is what is *not* in the image: **no fixed JDK**. The image carries a version manager, and each repository declares the exact Java and Maven it wants in a committed `.mise.toml`. Upgrading Java is therefore a commit in *your* repository — no image rebuild, no registry push, no platform release, and no ticket to the platform team — and two repositories can sit on two different Java versions at the same time without interfering. A repository that pins nothing gets the image's stated baseline (Java 21 + Maven 3.9) rather than a confusing `mvn: command not found`, and a repository that pinned for a *different* version manager is told so by name. The downloaded runtime — and your Maven dependency cache — live in the agent's own workspace, so they survive the container restarts that used to throw them away. For platform teams, adding the next stack is one Dockerfile and one manifest: `make toolchain-images` discovers every variant, and a build-time contract check fails the build if a variant drifts from the base agent image (a drifted variant builds cleanly and then fails hours later, mid-run, as "command not found"). Locked-down networks are covered up front — where to put a Nexus `settings.xml` and how to point the runtime downloader at a proxy — in the new `docs/agent-toolchains.md`, which is written for both audiences: the project team pinning a version, and the platform team shipping the image. (#1941)

- **Agent Chat: configure an agent from the chat (Settings tab)** — the right rail gains a **Settings** tab holding the agent's full configuration: display name, system prompt (including a team member's *Inherit* / *Additional input* / *Exclusive override* modes), skills, MCP overrides, plugin grants, host context folders and repositories with **Sync repositories**. It is the *same* form the Agents page's edit panel renders — one component, so a field added later appears in both and the two cannot drift — and it saves through the same `PUT /api/agents/:id`, so an identical edit produces an identical stored configuration. Plugin grants and context folders keep their immediate-apply semantics; in the chat a context-folder change additionally asks for confirmation first, because the container re-provision it triggers ends the run you are watching, and the panel then says so rather than leaving the chat looking frozen. A save reports that prompt and skill changes take effect on the agent's next run. Unsaved edits are **preserved per agent** for the rest of the session — switching agents and back finds the form as you left it, behind an "unsaved changes" banner with **Discard changes** — so a list click is never blocked by a modal and nothing is lost silently. The tab is hidden for a caller who may not manage the agent (the same boundary the server enforces). It normally follows the open chat, and a rail menu can point it elsewhere: **Team settings** on a team group header now opens the team's own form here instead of jumping to the Agents page, and **Settings** on an agent row reaches a disabled agent that has no chat to open. The Agents page's edit panel is unchanged. (#1929)

- **Agent Chat: manage agents without leaving the chat** — the chat's left rail now carries the lifecycle actions that used to require a trip to the Agents page. Every agent row offers a context menu (hover kebab or right-click, and `Menu`/`Shift+F10` from the keyboard) with **Enable/Disable**, **Clone**, **Export (ZIP)**, **Save as template** and the destructive **Reset to stub** / **Delete**; every team group header offers the team **Enable/Disable** fan-out (with the same partial-failure report), **Add member**, **Add \<role\> role** for a loop-closing role the team is missing, **Edit team**, **Export team**, **Open in Flow Editor** and **Delete team**. A **+** control in the rail header opens **New agent** or **Import agent**, and the imported agent joins the list without a page reload. Every action runs the *same* dialog, confirmation and request as its Agents-page counterpart — including the drain-vs-abort prompt when an agent is disabled mid-run — and an action a user may not perform is shown inert with an honest reason rather than failing at the server. A new **show disabled agents** toggle (off by default, remembered per user) lists paused agents so they can be re-enabled from the chat; they are marked disabled and cannot be opened as a chat until they are. Ordering, unread and busy indicators, environment labels, group collapse, the text filter and the `Alt+↑`/`Alt+↓` reorder are unchanged. (#1928)

- **Model Provider health monitoring** — the Model Providers page now shows a **Health** column for each provider, kept current by a background poller that probes every configured provider on a schedule (default every ~5 minutes) independently of anyone opening the page. Each cell shows the last-known status — **OK**, **Error** (with the failure reason), or **Unknown** when a provider has never been probed (never a guessed green) — and when it was last checked. Saving a provider re-probes it immediately so a freshly-broken endpoint is flagged on save. A persistently-failing endpoint is retried with exponential backoff rather than hammered every cycle. In Agent Chat, selecting an agent whose bound provider is currently unreachable shows a non-blocking amber warning banner, so a red endpoint is known before an agent is launched against it. First cut covers Model Providers; MCP servers and plugin brokers are a planned fast-follow. (#1843)

- **spec-kit plugin: spec-driven development via team grant** — spec-kit is now an installable Agent Studio plugin. System Admins install it once from the Plugins page (content is fetched from GitHub at install time and pinned to the exact version; the Plugins page records and displays the pinned version). Team Admins then grant it to an Agent Team — the grant applies immediately, without agent respawn, and provisions the `/speckit:specify`, `/speckit:plan`, `/speckit:tasks`, and `/speckit:implement` slash commands into the workspace. Revoking the grant removes those commands. Both greenfield (new repository) and brownfield (existing repository) runs are supported. (#1798)

- **Command-only plugins (platform capability)** — the plugin manifest now supports a "command-only" variant (`commands` field, no `sidecar`/`cli`). Plugins of this type provision Claude Code slash commands into the agent workspace on grant, using the same additive file sync as skill commands (#1796), but carry no broker sidecar and require no system credentials. spec-kit (#1798) is the first plugin of this kind. Content fetched from GitHub at install time is pinned and recorded in `installed-version.json` (`repo`, `ref`, `fetchedAt`); the version is surfaced on the Plugins page. (#1798)

- **Custom slash commands for agents (foundation)** — a granted skill can now carry Claude Code **slash commands** (a `commands/*.md` subdirectory). Its command files are provisioned into the agent workspace's `.claude/commands/` — namespaced per skill, invoked in chat as `/<skill>:<command> [args]` — as an additive file sync that applies without an agent respawn, exactly like the skill files themselves. A message that begins with `/` but matches no known command is passed through and answered as ordinary text (no crash). Each command's optional `description` is parsed and exposed via `GET /api/agents/:id/commands` for the composer autocomplete (shipped in #1797, below). Chat-message invocation only for now. (#1796)

- **Slash-command autocomplete in the composer** — typing `/` as the first character of an Agent Chat message now opens a filterable, keyboard-navigable palette of the selected agent's provisioned slash commands (name + description). Arrow keys move, Enter or Tab selects, Esc dismisses; picking one inserts `/<command> ` with the caret ready for arguments. A `/` typed mid-message never opens it, and an agent with no commands shows nothing. The list reflects the agent's actual `.claude/commands/` workspace tree, so a command added by hand appears alongside the skill-provisioned ones. (#1797)

- **Knowledge page: Review mode** — a guided, one-at-a-time triage flow over suggested knowledge candidates. Navigate with keyboard shortcuts (`P` promote · `M` add to merge · `D` dismiss · `→`/`Enter` skip · `Esc` close) and track progress via an "n of m" indicator. Accessible via **Start review** on the Suggested tab. (#787)

- **Multi-candidate knowledge merge** — an **Add to merge** basket lets you queue multiple suggestion cards (from the list or inside review mode), then promote them all into a single skill in one step. The resulting skill's Origin tab records every contributing lesson. (#787)

- **Skill rollout auto-reconcile** — the orchestrator now continuously detects team members who have been granted a skill but haven't received it yet (e.g. due to a prior sync failure) and re-syncs them automatically with exponential backoff and an attempt cap. The Rollout tab reflects live sync state, showing "syncing…" while in progress and surfacing the last error with a Retry button only after the loop reports a failure. (#787)

- **Stale candidate hygiene** — knowledge suggestions with no new supporting evidence for 4 weeks (configurable via `KNOWLEDGE_SUGGESTION_STALE_WEEKS`) are automatically moved to a `stale` state. Stale candidates are hidden from the default inbox view but accessible via a new **Stale** filter, can be manually restored, and are automatically revived if fresh evidence re-surfaces them. (#787)

- **Knowledge domain taxonomy** — teams now maintain a persistent list of knowledge domains (e.g. "Security", "Onboarding"), seeded automatically from the team's role templates. Operators can add, rename, merge, split, or remove domains via the new **Domains panel** in the Knowledge page, which also shows the current skill budget. Domain changes emit feed events for audit. Teams with no confirmed domains are unaffected. (#790)

- **Cluster routing against domains** — when a converged knowledge cluster matches an existing domain, it is now routed as an **amendment** to that domain's skill rather than spawning a new micro-skill. Clusters that don't match any domain accumulate until they meet a mass threshold, at which point a budget-aware **new-domain proposal** is surfaced for operator review. This significantly reduces skill sprawl. (#790)

- **LLM skill synthesis** — teams can opt in (`synthesizeSkills` curation config) to have SKILL.md content generated by the LLM in a structured playbook format (sections: *When to use / The flow / Rules / Gotchas / Examples*). When synthesis fails, a visible `[needs synthesis]` marker is written and the item is re-queued automatically. Off by default; the existing heuristic draft is written verbatim when disabled. (#790)

- **Skill distillation** — after a configurable number of amendments (default 5, `distillAfterAmendments`), a skill can be re-synthesized to deduplicate, restructure, and re-abstract its content while preserving all provenance entry IDs exactly. A **Distill** action with an old-vs-new diff modal is available in both the skill inspector (Knowledge page) and the Skills Registry. At autonomy level 3 distillation runs automatically; otherwise it surfaces a diff for review before applying. An unusable LLM result leaves the skill byte-identical — the skill is never corrupted on failure. (#796)

- **Consolidation migration** — existing micro-skills and open candidates can be migrated into consolidated domain-based playbooks in a resumable, per-domain process. Each step uses the existing merge machinery (atomic grant swap, lineage chaining, source archival), so grants are never briefly broken during migration. Remaining candidates after consolidation are automatically re-routed through the standard domain classifier. (#796)

- **Domain role surfacing** — skill cards now display the roles a domain applies to (e.g. "for: developer, reworker"), derived from the domain's role list. (#796)

- **Token usage: average cost per work item** — the Token Usage page's Work items tab now shows three summary cards — **Ø total / work item**, **Ø input / work item**, and **Ø output / work item** — so stakeholders can see the mean token cost of one work item at a glance without exporting data. Cards update automatically when the date range changes or the page is refreshed. Zero work items shows `—` rather than an error. (#797)

- **Team-scoped SSH keys and GitHub tokens** — credentials can now be owned by a specific team (`team` scope) or shared across teams by a System Admin (`system` scope). Team Admins gain a self-service credential management page where they can create and delete SSH keys and GitHub tokens for their own team without involving a System Admin. System Admins can additionally assign a system-scoped credential to any set of teams. The SSH Keys and GitHub Tokens pages are now accessible to Team Admins, and the Working Environment credential pickers show exactly the credentials a Team Admin is permitted to see. (#803)

- **Credential rename and reassignment** — System Admins can rename any credential and move it between teams or change its scope (team → system or vice versa) via a new **Edit** action on the credential table. Reassigning a credential that is referenced by an existing Working Environment is blocked with a clear error (409) if the move would leave the environment without access. (#803)

- **Team-scoped Model Providers** — the Model Providers page now follows the same scope model as SSH keys and GitHub tokens. Team Admins can self-serve model providers for their team; System Admins can register system-scoped providers and assign them to specific teams. Each team sees only the providers it has been given access to. Existing providers are normalized to team-scoped on read — no data migration required. The Model Providers nav entry is now accessible to Team Admins; System Admins continue to have full visibility. (#806)

- **Token Usage: per-team filter** — System Admins can now filter the Token Usage page (Summary and Work Items tabs, including CSV exports) by team. Selecting a specific team shows only that team's records; "All teams" remains the default and includes legacy entries with no team assigned. (#810)

- **Consolidation migration: operator UI** — the Knowledge page now includes a **Consolidation panel** that walks operators through folding accumulated micro-skills and open candidates into confirmed domain playbooks. The panel opens with a preview showing how many skills and candidates collapse per domain, then steps through each domain one at a time: **Review playbook** synthesises a draft for approval, **Accept & merge** applies it (archives all sources with full lineage, swaps grants atomically), and **Skip** defers a domain without applying changes. Migration state is resumable — reopening the panel continues from the first undecided domain. Remaining unmatched candidates are routed automatically at the end of the run. The panel updates live via the knowledge feed as merges complete. (#842)

- **Domains panel: AI-powered domain proposal** — a new **Propose with AI** action suggests 5–8 domain names and scopes derived from the team's lesson corpus; proposals are shown un-persisted for review and only applied on **Confirm**. When no LLM target is configured, a deterministic role-seed fallback is used and flagged with a visible warning. An over-budget nudge in both the domain list and the Add field steers operators toward merging existing domains before creating new ones. (#841)

- **Structured outcomes port for agent roles (data model / API)** — process template roles and live agents now support a structured `outcomes` field: a named decision tree where each option maps a condition to one or more actions (`setLabel`, `openPr`, `openIssue`). When authored, `outcomes` supersedes the flat `emits` list and generates the same graph edges plus an `outcome` id tag for traceability. Process templates round-trip `outcomes` through materialisation, member configs, and export/import (with `{{var}}` substitution in label names and conditions). `PUT /api/agents/:id` with `outcomes` persists it and clears any existing `emits`. UI editing and runtime prompt injection are deferred to a later phase. (#827)

- **Flow Editor: Routing tab and outcome switch ports** — the node inspector now has a dedicated **Routing tab** that consolidates, in one place, the agent's incoming label-hand-off listeners (with their contributing producers) and its outgoing outcomes. Operators can author a structured decision switch directly in the tab: choose between *exactly one* (exclusive) and *any of* (parallel) mode, add/edit/remove named outcome options (each with an optional `when` condition and one or more actions — `setLabel`, `openPr`, `openIssue`), and confirm with **Save**. A **Suggest outcomes…** action generates a draft set of outcomes via LLM (or a keyword heuristic fallback); proposals are held unconfirmed until Save so nothing persists on decline. On the canvas, an `exactlyOne` node gains a **◇ switch badge** and one labelled draggable output port per outcome; dragging a port pre-fills the hand-off dialog with the outcome's label. Outcome-keyed edges render in amber titled *◇ \<outcome\>*; clicking one focuses the Routing tab on that outcome. Channel fan-in keying is updated so a single producer can feed one consumer via differently-labelled channels without collision. Routing tab and canvas editing work identically on drafts and live teams. (#828)

- **Structured outcomes now auto-generate agent routing instructions** — when a role or agent has a structured `outcomes` declaration, the system automatically generates a `## Outcomes` block in the agent's prompt. Each outcome option is rendered in plain language: a title, an optional `when:` condition, and a "Then:" action clause describing the label to apply, the PR or issue to open, or the notification to send. The block is idempotent (stripped and regenerated on every edit), coexists cleanly with the existing "open a PR/issue" prompt block without duplicating instructions, and is applied at both materialisation time and eagerly on every live outcomes edit. Legacy agents using only flat `emits` are unaffected — no block is generated for them. (#829)

- **Speech-to-text voice input in agent chat** — the message input now includes a microphone button that lets you dictate messages using speech recognition. Click to start recording; your speech is transcribed and inserted into the text field in real time. The button appears only on browsers that support the Speech Recognition API. (#852)

- **GCP VM pilot: deploy via GitHub Actions or manual scripts** — the platform can now be hosted on a Google Cloud Platform virtual machine. A GitHub Actions workflow automates deployment on push, and manual deploy scripts support ad-hoc releases. The admin console runs on port 14000 in this hosting mode. (#890)

- **Per-channel and per-input wake-prompt overrides** — each GitHub channel and data-flow input in the Flow Editor's Routing tab can now carry an optional wake-prompt override. When set, this text replaces the auto-derived prompt that the consuming agent receives when that specific channel or input fires, letting operators tailor the agent's starting context per connection without changing its overall system prompt. (#868)

- **Per-agent model provider chip and in-chat switch** — the agent chat header now displays a chip showing the model provider currently active for that agent (name and auth type). Clicking the chip opens a popover where operators can switch the agent's provider on the spot, without navigating to the agent's settings page. The active provider is also shown in the Session Log next to each Claude invocation for full transparency. (#904)

- **Agent Chat Sessions: default to the most recent finished session** — the Session Log panel now opens directly on the most recently completed session rather than on the live (still-writing) session. This makes it faster to review what an agent just did after a trigger run completes. The live session remains accessible via the session picker. (#910)

- **New `loop` trigger type: governed continuation loops** — agent trigger configurations can now include a `loop` trigger that runs the agent's prompt back-to-back, chaining iterations automatically until a stop condition is met. Two stop conditions are supported: `until.command` (a shell command whose exit 0 signals "done") and `maxIterations` (required; a hard cap that prevents unbounded loops). Iterations are strictly sequential with an optional `minGapSeconds` pause between them. When a loop reaches its terminal state, the agent posts a single outcome DM reporting the reason (done vs. exhausted), iterations consumed, and the last `until` output. Maintenance Mode is honoured — the loop pauses inside a maintenance window and resumes with one catch-up iteration after. (#942)

- **Loop trigger: per-iteration verifier gate** — each iteration of a `loop` trigger can now be gated by a `verify` step that must pass before the iteration counts as progress. Two verification modes are available: `verify.command` (a shell command — exit 0 means pass; on failure, the command's output is fed back into the next iteration's prompt as corrective context) and `verify.agentId` (routes the iteration's output to another agent for a `[PASS]`/`[FAIL]` verdict before proceeding). Fail-closed: an undeliverable, timed-out, or unparseable verdict counts as a failure, never as a silent pass. For team loop agents the verifier defaults to a sibling role (preferring `*-reviewer` or `quality-gate` roles) when none is specified, and team agents are prevented from self-grading. Standalone agents may opt in to self-verification with `verify.selfGraded`. (#947)

- **Loop trigger: output-token budget and runaway backstop surfacing** — an optional `maxTokens` field on a `loop` trigger sets a total output-token budget for the run; when the cumulative token spend reaches the cap the loop stops with outcome `exhausted` and the terminal DM reports tokens spent vs budget alongside iterations used. The existing runaway spawn-backstop (#611) is now surfaced as a distinct `paused` outcome with a ⏸️ heading and an explanation, rather than silently dropping the iteration. (#949)

- **Loop trigger: UI authoring in trigger form and canvas** — the `loop` trigger type is now available in both trigger-authoring surfaces: the chat right-rail Triggers panel and the Flows canvas edge dialog (via the shared `TriggerForm`). The form exposes all loop fields: `maxIterations` (required — save is blocked without a positive whole number), `until.command` (stop condition), `verify` (none, command, or agent picker — team members get a sibling reviewer pre-filled by default with separation enforced server-side), `maxTokens`, and `minGapSeconds`. Loop trigger rows render a distinct ↻ badge and a compact stop-contract summary line. (#952)

- **`github-event` triggers: declarative claim labels** — triggers can now carry an optional `claim: { remove, add }` field that instructs the runtime to swap labels on the triggering issue or PR. The swap executes after the preCheck gate but before the Claude invoke, so the work item's label reflects actual agent state — making three previously identical-looking stall modes externally distinguishable: *never started* (wake label still set), *died mid-run* (claim label stuck), and *finished without an outcome* (claim label gone, no outcome label). On double failure the claim is automatically rolled back — the claim label is removed and the wake label is restored — which re-emits the event through the poller and restarts the chain event-natively without a separate reconciler. The swap is best-effort: a failure logs the error with full stderr output but does not block the run. (#974)

- **Claim labels: auto-derivation, schema enforcement, canvas badge, and opt-out** — building on the runtime executor (#974), every label listener derived from a channel port (by the flow weaver or live reconcilers) is now automatically stamped with a `claim` field following the enforced naming convention `{ remove: "<labelFilter>", add: "<labelFilter>:wip" }`. Deviations from the convention are hard validation errors so that the Layer 3 stall-detection probe (#959) can reliably compute the expected claim label without per-trigger configuration. Channels can opt out by setting `autoClaim: false` on the port — the flag survives reconcile because it lives on the port, not on the ephemeral derived trigger. The Process Canvas shows a claim badge on listener edges and surfaces the derived claim in the Connections panel with an enable/disable toggle; the displayed value is always read from the server-derived trigger, so the `:wip` convention has exactly one implementation. A new advisory `unclaimed-listener` lint flags hand-authored label listeners that carry no claim. (#977)

- **Flow-anomaly detector: deterministic stall detection and observe panel** — the Process Canvas gains an **Anomalies** toolbar button (live team scope only) that runs an on-demand sweep over the team's open issues and PRs and classifies four stall classes with age: `wake-not-claimed` (wake label present past T₁ = 15 min with no claim and no run), `claim-stalled` (claim label stuck past T₂ = 2 h with no branch/PR/commit activity), `output-missing` (agent verifiably ran but its declared outgoing label or PR never appeared), and `gate-idle` (human gate pending past T₃ = 24 h, advisory). Results appear in a side panel listing severity dot, class, object link, age, message, and responsible agent; a count badge is shown on the toolbar button and on each affected canvas node. The thresholds T₁/T₂/T₃ are configurable per team via `anomalyThresholds` on the team-agent config (defaults apply when absent). The sweep is observe-only — no automatic repair in this phase — and degrades gracefully: a failed fetch produces a partial result with an `errors[]` list rather than taking down the Flows view. Skipped entirely during Maintenance Mode. (#993)

### Bug Fixes

- **A push trigger now wakes on the first push to a new branch, the one that opens the pull request** — a `github-event` trigger listening to `push` used to miss exactly that push and only fire on later pushes to the same branch, because GitHub's Events API (what the poller reads) reports no push event for a branch-creating push. The poller now treats the pull request being opened (or reopened) from a branch in the same repository as that first push: a `push` trigger fires once for it, and the GitHub Event Log shows a delivered `push` row for the branch and head commit. The same commit never wakes the trigger twice — if GitHub later does report a real push for the same branch and commit, it is logged as a dropped duplicate. Triggers listening to `pull_request.opened` are unaffected, and a trigger listening to both only fires once, on the pull request. The usual history-horizon, throttle and burst-digest protections apply. A branch pushed without opening a pull request still isn't detected, since GitHub reports nothing for it. (#2529)
- **Agents can once again put a decision to you as a clickable quick-reply card instead of burying it in prose** — the question card in Agent Chat had effectively stopped appearing: headless agent runs never had Claude Code's built-in question tool, and the bundled server that used to re-supply it is refused by the enterprise policy that blocks MCP servers, so it was switched off and agents fell back to asking in plain text that nothing could detect or route. Agents now ask through a small built-in command, `aee-ask` (e.g. `aee-ask "Which base branch?" --header Branch --option "main::default" --option "release::hotfix line"`), which needs no MCP at all. The question is posted as the last message of the agent's turn, with the same option buttons as before; clicking one continues the agent's session with your choice, exactly as it used to. The agent's turn ends the moment it asks, so nothing more runs behind your back and the question stays the last thing you see. A question that allows several answers (`--multi`) now gets a real multi-select card: tick any number of options, then press **Submit**, and the agent continues with every option you picked (previously such questions only ever showed as text with a "you can pick more than one" hint). Single-choice cards still answer with one click, as before. Every agent's instructions now tell it to use `aee-ask` rather than asking in prose. (#2523)
- **A pooled role could quietly fill up with finished or stuck instances and stop accepting new tickets** — a pool instance was only ever reaped when its ticket closed (via the flow's own ticket-limit reconcile), so a role/flow configured with no ticket limit got no reaping at all: finished, idle, and stuck instances all lingered forever, eventually pinning every slot and refusing new dispatches. Idle instances (finished their run, ticket still open) are now reaped on their own — quietly, no timeout label — after 30 minutes idle by default; a stuck/crashed instance is still flagged for a human and reaped after 2 hours by default; both timeouts, plus how long an idle instance's archived session log is kept (7 days by default), now apply out of the box and no longer require a ticket limit to be configured at all. Archived pool session logs, previously never cleaned up, are now pruned once they age past that retention window. (#2301)
- **Model Providers: an Anthropic-compatible endpoint with no `/v1/models` route (e.g. Azure AI Foundry) no longer reports as broken** — **Test connection**, **Discover models**, and the background health poller all probed only `GET /v1/models`, so a gateway that serves the Messages API but exposes no model-listing route failed the connection test, showed unhealthy in the poller, and returned a raw HTTP 404 from Discover models — even though it works fine for real inference. On a 404/405 from `/v1/models`, all three now fall back to a minimal `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens` against an intentionally-invalid placeholder model: a `400` (or `2xx`) means the endpoint is reachable and authenticated, just without a model catalog; `401`/`403` is a real auth failure; `404` on that route too means the Base URL itself is wrong. No real inference ever runs against the placeholder model, so the fallback is free. Discover models now tells you to enter the model name(s) manually instead of showing an empty list or a raw error. Providers whose `/v1/models` already works are unaffected. (#2306)
- **Writing into the global skill pool now follows one permission rule on every path, not three different ones — and two of those paths had no check at all** — `Import` and `Merge` still required System Admin outright, a rule left over from before #2178/#2180 decided that entering the global pool is a Team-Admin act; `+ New Skill` and editing a skill's content (`POST /api/skills`, `PUT /api/skills/:name`, `PUT /api/skills/:name/file`) had **no permission check whatsoever** — any signed-in user could create or overwrite a global skill outright. All four now require System Admin or Team Admin of any team for a global target, matching `Promote to global` and `Publish`; writing into a specific team's pool is unchanged, always Team Admin of that team. A refusal also now names the actual action (`import`, `create`, `edit`) instead of always saying "transfer". The Skills Registry's **Import to:** and **+ New Skill** target pickers now only offer a pool the signed-in caller may actually write to. (#2307)

- **A team's Skills tab now actually does what it says: checking or unchecking a skill and clicking Save Changes reaches every already-running member immediately, not just the team's config file** — the tab's own help text has long promised that a member "inherits [the team's skills] unless they override," copied into its workspace at (re)seed time, but the endpoint behind **Save Changes** (`PUT /api/agents/:id`) only wrote the team's `skills[]` to disk and returned; nothing copied the new skill into, or removed the old one from, any member's live workspace. A member could not use a newly checked skill, and kept the files of an unchecked one, until something unrelated — disabling and re-enabling that one agent — happened to trigger a fresh seed. This silently contradicted the tab's own explanation for any team that already had members, which is the common case. A second, unrelated mechanism already did this correctly: the Knowledge page's per-skill activate/deactivate toggle copies or removes a skill's files into every non-overriding member's workspace, rebuilds its `CLAUDE.md`, and mirrors the change into an isolated volume — with no container restart and no session loss — but the bulk Skills-tab save was never wired to it. It now is: saving the tab diffs the submitted set against the team's current one and reuses that exact sync, one call per added or removed skill, so both surfaces now give the identical guarantee. A member with its own `skillsOverride` (including an explicit empty one) is unaffected either way, a stub member still picks up the team's current set at its own first seed, and a save that doesn't actually change the active set does no work. (#2286)
- **QuickStart: the Agent Name field is no longer silently ignored when a GitHub Repository URL is entered** — in the "Chat with one assistant" wizard, typing a custom name and also pasting a repository URL used to submit successfully but discard the typed name: the created agent was always named after the repository (`<repo> Agent`), with nothing in the form indicating the field would be dropped. Submitting without a repository URL worked correctly, which masked the bug for anyone who tested only that path. The Agent Name field is now the **only** source of the agent's display name on both paths, with no repository-derived fallback — the repository path now requires a name exactly as the repo-less path always has, and refuses submission with the same "Enter a name for your assistant." message when it is left blank. Model Provider is unaffected and stays out of scope: on the repository path the agent still runs on the platform's default provider regardless of the form's selection, which the `create` help topic's *"Why Name and Model Provider are not used here"* chapter now states correctly for Model Provider alone. (#2222)
- **Agent Chat: selecting a team no longer shows a blank right rail** — clicking a team's header row opened the ordinary agent tab strip (Sessions / Workspace / Triggers / Knowledge) with an empty body underneath, because the right rail had no way to tell "a team is open" apart from "nothing has loaded yet" — both looked like the same null agent id to it. The rail now knows which one it is: selecting a team narrows it to the **Settings** tab alone (or to no tabs at all for a caller who may not manage that team), and a tab left over from before the subject became a team is forced back onto Settings rather than rendering an empty panel behind the scenes. (#2216)
- **Creating a quick assistant no longer fails on an agent-id collision** — `POST /api/quickstart` (the repository fast lane) and `POST /api/quickstart/llm` (the repo-less path) derive the new agent's id from the repository URL or the chosen name, but the check they used to decide whether that id was free was weaker than the check that actually refused it, and advisory rather than authoritative: an id whose directory had already been removed but whose background teardown was still in flight read as free to the console and as taken to the orchestrator, so the request landed a raw, unactionable *"Agent '\<id\>' already exists"* naming an agent the user could not even see. The orchestrator now resolves and reserves the first free id itself — `<base>`, `<base>-2`, `<base>-3`, … — at the exact point it creates the agent, using the strictest existing predicate (a persisted config, an on-disk directory, or an in-flight teardown) and reserving the chosen id synchronously before any provisioning starts, so two concurrent requests for the same repository can no longer collide. A collision is never shown as a warning or an error: the only visible difference is the id of the resulting assistant, which the success card and the redirect to chat now both reflect. The dead *"Agent reused"* success branch (unreachable since #2071 always creates a fresh agent) is also removed from the QuickStart page. (#2217)
- **Configuring an agent manually now suggests a free id instead of leaving you to guess one** — where the fast lane picks its own id, this form asks you to type one, so the previous fix could not apply here: a taken id still landed as a bare *"Agent '\<id\>' already exists"* with no id field visible to change and no hint at what would work. The refusal now names a free alternative — continuing an existing numeric suffix rather than doubling it, so a taken `my-agent-2` suggests `my-agent-3`, never `my-agent-2-2` — computed by the same strict check the fast lane's own resolution uses, so it can never suggest an id that would turn out to be taken after all (for example one whose teardown is still quietly finishing in the background). The suggestion appears inline next to the id field with a one-click **Use this id**, disappears the moment you edit the field to something else so it can never go stale, and accepting it is treated exactly like typing it yourself — so a later edit to the display name will not silently overwrite it. The id you actually submit is always re-checked at that point rather than trusted from the earlier refusal. Clone, import and the quickstart fast lane are unaffected. (#2218)
- **A label hand-off no longer wakes an agent twice for the same piece of work** — a `github-event` trigger that waits for a label (say a developer on `ready`) was matched against the *whole* set of labels currently on the ticket, not just the one label the incoming event added. That match was introduced on purpose, because GitHub's Events API collapses several labels added in one action into a single event that names only one of them, and matching the full set is the only way not to miss a wake whose label was collapsed away. The side effect was that **any** later label change on the same ticket re-fired every waiting agent whose label still happened to be there — including the agent's **own** claim swap (when it flips `ready` to `ready:wip` to show work in progress, the two are not applied atomically, so the "added `ready:wip`" event still lists `ready` for a moment), and including plain churn of an unrelated label like `next-wave`. The existing guard against the claim swap keyed on the single label the event named, which the Events API reports unreliably, so it missed exactly this case: the developer picked up a ticket, and three minutes later picked up the identical ticket again, did the work again, and both runs reported `[PASS]`. The wake is now **edge-triggered**: a label match fires only when that label has *newly* appeared on the ticket since the platform last acted on it, and is re-armed only once the label is genuinely removed (or the ticket closes), so a real remove-and-re-add still fires as it should. This is immune to unrelated label churn, to the agent's own claim swap, to a slow start while the run waits its turn, and to the Events API mis-reporting which label changed — because it no longer trusts any single event's named label, only the ticket's label history across polls. The state is remembered on disk so a restart cannot forget an in-flight wake and re-open the double-fire. The collapsed-multi-add recovery it was built on still works — the *first* appearance of a collapsed label fires exactly as before. A kill-switch (`AEE_WAKE_EDGE_DEDUP=0`) restores the previous behaviour if ever needed. (#2152)
- **Create team dialog: the submit button now looks as unavailable as it is** — the primary button in the Materialize dialog (and its **Retry** / **Add missing agents** siblings) carried an explicit inline `background`, so a `<button disabled>` picked up none of the browser's default greying — an inert button looked pixel-identical to a live one, hand cursor included. All three now share one `primaryStyle(disabled)` helper applying the same `opacity`/`cursor` house pattern used elsewhere in the console (the Flow Editor's toolbar button, the Team-Setup wizard's own primary button). The disabled reason is unified too: instead of a separate `envBlocking` boolean deciding whether the button was clickable while `validateLaunch` explained something else entirely, one `launchBlockReason` now drives both — settled on the team id first, so a trailing dash mid-edit does not flicker the message — and it shows as persistent text next to the button rather than only in a hover title. A related bug in the same dialog is fixed alongside it: a failed `/api/environments` load used to be swallowed into an empty list, rendering the honest *"No working environment exists yet"* message for someone who actually has environments but could not reach them right now; the failure now surfaces its own error instead. (#2157)

- **Agent Chat's "Trigger fired…" notice and the Pending queue now name the label that actually woke the agent, not merely a label that happened to be on the same ticket** — when a human applied several labels to an issue in one action (for example `flow-control` and `ready` together), the GitHub Events API collapses that into a single event naming only one of them, arbitrarily. The trigger notice and the Pending queue both read that raw, arbitrary label straight off the event, so a `ready` gate could show as `flow-control` — a wrong answer exactly where someone looks when they are already unsure why an agent is running. The GitHub Event Log's outcome chip was fixed the same way for the same reason previously (#1846); this closes the other two places the identical mistake was made independently. The label that actually matched a subscriber's filter is now stamped onto the queued work item at the moment it is decided — by the poller, by an internal agent-to-agent hand-off, and by an operator's manual reassignment (re-stamped for the *target*, never carried over from the source) — and both surfaces read that stamp first, falling back to the trigger's own filter and finally to the raw event label only for an entry queued before this fix or a listener that reacts to any label. A second, unrelated defect in the same notice is fixed alongside it: a flow-generated trigger's auto-generated id (e.g. `— t1785499525109689`) had crept back into the notice text after an earlier ticket removed it deliberately — it is gone again. (#2151)

- **A deleted person's GitHub token can no longer keep signing their agents' work, and a failed offboarding can no longer look like a clean deletion** — the previous release restored the offboarding steps that had been dead since August, and said plainly that what they had already missed was not cleaned up retroactively. This is that cleanup, plus the safety net that makes the whole class of failure survivable. **Three things change.** First, **a personal credential whose owner no longer exists stops working.** Until now the runtime matched a personal SSH key or GitHub token on its owner's id alone and never asked whether that person still had an account — so a departed colleague's token was found, was perfectly valid, and their agents carried on committing, pushing and opening pull requests under their name with no warning anywhere. An attributed write on behalf of somebody who is no longer a user is now **refused**, with a message that says what actually has to happen (finish the offboarding, and **revoke the credential at GitHub**) rather than the usual advice to add a personal credential — nobody can add one for a deleted account. Ordinary read-only work such as cloning and polling is unaffected, because it legitimately runs under the team credential. The important property is that this holds **regardless of whether offboarding ran**: it is the containment layer, not a second copy of the teardown. Second, **the platform now finishes the offboardings it missed.** At startup it compares the credential stores against the user list, and for every deleted owner still holding a personal credential or a running agent it replays the *same* offboarding a deletion performs — drain their agents, delete their personal SSH keys and GitHub tokens. It logs each orphaned credential first, with its fingerprint, because **deleting the local copy is not revocation**: those tokens were live and in automated use for up to four weeks and must be treated as exposed, so revoke each one at GitHub using the fingerprints in that log. The pass is idempotent and finds nothing on every boot after the first; if the user list cannot be read it refuses to run at all rather than acting on a list where everyone looks deleted. Third, **a deletion whose offboarding fails now says so.** It used to log one line to the server console and report the deletion as clean — which is exactly what hid the original bug for four weeks. The response now always states what the offboarding did, a failure or a partial run (an agent that could not be paused) is named, and the degradation is recorded in the **GitHub Event Log** where an operator can see it. The deletion itself still completes: refusing it would leave you with a user who is still present, whose credentials are still present, and whose offboarding you have no other way to finish — and the credential refusal above already contains the damage either way. One limitation is worth stating: because a deleted user leaves no record behind, **how long ago each departure happened cannot be recovered** — the log reports each orphaned credential's creation date, which is the closest evidence the stack still holds. (#2138)

- **Creating a user no longer reports a failure for a user it just created — and deleting one now actually offboards them** — pressing **Create user** on the Administration page showed the red banner `orchestrator is not defined` and left the dialog open over an unchanged list, while the record had in fact already been written to disk; the only way to find that out was to reload the page. The message was not a refusal of anything you typed, it was a JavaScript scoping bug: three places in the user-administration routes reached for a handle that does not exist there, and the create path had no error handling around it, so an internal fault became a 500 and the raw engine message travelled all the way into the dialog. Creating a user now returns cleanly, closes the dialog and refreshes the list. **The more damaging half was silent.** The same mistake sat in the two teardown steps that run when a user is deleted, both inside a `try`/`catch` — so since August they had been failing invisibly on every deletion: the departing person's agents were never paused and their personal SSH keys and GitHub tokens were never deleted, and their Matrix account was never deactivated, leaving a live account and a live access token behind for a user the platform had removed. Both now run. If you have deleted users on this stack, their Matrix accounts and personal credentials are still there and are not cleaned up retroactively — deactivate them in the Conduwuit admin room and remove the credentials from the credential pages. Two pieces of hardening ride along so this class of failure cannot look the same again: work that happens *after* a record has been persisted can no longer turn a completed write into a reported failure, and a raw internal error message is no longer rendered verbatim in a dialog — an unclassified server fault now says so in plain language and keeps the technical string in the browser console for a bug report, while every refusal written for a person (a name that is taken, a last administrator who cannot be removed) is still shown to you exactly as the server worded it. (#2135)

- **The sidebar's Agent Chat entry no longer scrolls out of view** — on a short viewport, or with several navigation groups expanded, scrolling the sidebar carried the **Agent Chat** entry off the top while the **+ Create** button above it stayed put. Agent Chat is the console's most-used view, so it should behave like Create and never leave the screen. It now does: the ungrouped top tier is rendered *outside* the scrolling area, pinned directly beneath **+ Create**, and only the **Agent Setup / Connections / Administration** accordions (and the dynamic **Plugins** group) scroll below it. Nothing else about the entry changed — it keeps its unread and busy badges, its dot indicator in the collapsed icon rail, and its highlight while you are on a chat route, because it is still drawn by the same code as every other navigation row; the accordions keep their expand/collapse behaviour and their per-user collapse memory; and the spacing between Create, Agent Chat and the first group header is unchanged. The whole top tier is pinned rather than Agent Chat specifically, so a second always-visible entry added later inherits the behaviour instead of quietly scrolling away. (#2035)

- **Deleting a team no longer leaves orphaned agents behind** — a team member whose parent team is gone is not a working agent: its system prompt, skills and plugin grant are all inherited from the team, so what remains cannot run as intended, and it also occupies the member id a same-named team would want to reuse. Team deletion now reliably takes every member with it — materialized members and *Not initialised* stubs alike — and it does so even when the team's own configuration is damaged, so a deletion that was interrupted halfway finishes the job when it is repeated instead of stranding the members it never reached. A member that genuinely could not be removed (a busy container, a Matrix user that would not deactivate) is now **reported**: the delete answers with the same `{ id, kind, error }` warning shape team creation already uses for its rollback, and both delete surfaces name the leftovers instead of showing a clean success. For installations that already carry orphans — from before this fix, or from a hand-edited parent id naming a team that never existed — there is a **System-Admin-only one-off cleanup**: `GET /api/agents/orphans` lists exactly what would be removed (id, materialized-or-stub, and *why* it is orphaned) and changes nothing, `POST /api/agents/orphans/cleanup` removes them, re-planning and logging the identical listing before it deletes anything. It is idempotent, so a second run is harmless and an interrupted sweep needs no special handling, and it is operator-invoked rather than automatic on startup — the platform does not delete agents without an explicit decision. It deletes rather than adopts: converting an orphan into a standalone agent was considered and rejected, because everything that made it useful came from the team. One class is deliberately reported but never removed — a member whose parent id names an agent that *does* exist but is not a team (a typo, or a hand-edited config): its parent is still there to point at, so correcting the id is a human decision rather than something a sweep should settle by deleting the member. With the cause fixed, the Agents page's "Orphaned agents" warning block no longer lists team members and gets **no replacement surface** anywhere else; the reason is recorded in the Agents help topic and in `docs/agent-roles.md` so the Helper Chat can answer where the list went. The block itself survives only for an Orchestrator's mission agents, whose orphans have a different cause and are handled with the rest of the BriefingPack removal. (#1960)

- **The console no longer blanks to a white page when something fails to render** — a single unexpected message, tool result or event could take down the entire view, losing the whole session and forcing a reload. The cause was structural rather than any one payload: the console had only two error boundaries, both wrapping just the markdown body of a chat message, so a throw anywhere else unmounted everything up to the page root. We had shipped fixes for individual triggers (#1857, #506, #481) and the blank page kept returning with a new one. Three containment layers now make it survivable regardless of trigger: the page area is wrapped so a page-level failure shows a recoverable card — **Try again · Go back · Reload page** — while the sidebar navigation stays usable, and navigating to another page clears it automatically; each chat transcript row and each session-log row is wrapped individually, so one malformed event degrades to a small *"This message could not be displayed."* placeholder while the rest of the chat keeps rendering and streaming; and every contained error is now reported to the backend (`POST /api/client-errors` → `/workspace/client-errors/*.jsonl` plus a `[client-error]` line in the console log the Logs page already streams), deduplicated and rate-limited so a re-render loop cannot flood it. The report deliberately carries only the error message, component stack, route and agent/session identifiers — never message bodies or agent output. The existing markdown boundaries are unchanged and remain the innermost layer. (#1974)

- **"Materialize agents now" is no longer ignored when a team is launched** — the checkbox on the launch step offered a choice the backend never read: both launch surfaces (the Team-Setup wizard and the Flows canvas' Materialize dialog) submit to the streaming create endpoint, and that handler left materialisation to its default, so **every** member was fully provisioned — Matrix identity, repository clone, running container — however the box was set. For a process with a dozen roles that meant a dozen clones nobody asked for, and the deferred path was reachable only by calling the non-streaming endpoint directly. The flag is now honoured on both paths. With the box ticked — the Team-Setup wizard's default — nothing changes: the behaviour is exactly what it was before, including for a caller that sends no flag at all. With it left off — the Materialize dialog's default — the members are created as **stubs**: their prompts, session policy and triggers are written and they appear in the team as *Not initialised*, but nothing is cloned and nothing is running until you enable one, which then provisions it in full. The progress output no longer claims work it did not do (a stub's step reads *Create member (stub)*, and the "Prepare agent conversations" phase is not announced at all, since a stub has no chat room yet), and the result card says the team was created as stubs rather than calling it "ready". Rollback, the label-suffix stamping and extend mode are unchanged, and a stub carries the same configuration it would have been given had it been materialised immediately. (#1955)

- **Agent chat: trigger-fired notices now show a consistent work-item link for both Issues and PRs** — the "⚡ Trigger fired … starting work…" notice showed an issue's number, title and link but frequently showed none of them for a pull request (and for any label hand-off delivered over the internal channel or the human-label fast-path). Two causes are fixed: the display was all-or-nothing (a missing title dropped the number and link too), and internally-delivered events carried no title. Issue and PR notices now use one identical format, always carrying the work item as an abbreviated markdown link `[#N](url)` (the full raw URL is no longer printed inline), with `: <title>` appended when it can be resolved. When the title cannot be resolved the notice still shows `#N` + link instead of collapsing to nothing. Internal-transport and plugin-fast-path deliveries now resolve the title with a best-effort GitHub fetch, so their notices carry it too; a failed lookup degrades gracefully rather than blocking the hand-off. (#1897)

- **spec-kit plugin now seeds a runnable project, not just the slash commands** — installing/granting spec-kit previously provisioned only the raw `/speckit:*` command files, which still contained unresolved `{SCRIPT}`/`{ARGS}` placeholders and depended on a `.specify/` scaffold (scripts, templates, memory) that was never created — so every command aborted with a "scaffold/template-resolver missing" error. The install-time fetch now (a) resolves the command placeholders exactly as `specify init --ai claude --script sh` would (`{SCRIPT}` → `.specify/scripts/bash/<name>.sh`, `{ARGS}` → `$ARGUMENTS`, and `__SPECKIT_COMMAND_<NAME>__` command cross-references → the `/speckit:<name>` invocation) and (b) fetches the `.specify/` scaffold alongside the commands; the grant seeds that scaffold into the agent's workspace root (with a `SPECIFY_INIT_DIR` pin so the scripts resolve the project deterministically). The install now fails loudly (and rolls back) if any placeholder is left unresolved, rather than seeding a broken command. The pinned version moved from the moving `main` branch to the `v1.0.3` tag. Revoking the grant removes the scaffold. (#1865)

- **Auto-curated skills now show descriptions in the agent Skills section** — skills created by the knowledge curator were written without YAML frontmatter, causing agents to see only the skill name with no indication of what the skill covers. The curator now writes a `name` + `description` frontmatter block into every generated SKILL.md. Existing skills are backfilled automatically the next time they are amended. (#807)

- **Process Canvas: "Add agent" button now shows a busy state during provisioning** — clicking "Add agent" on a live team previously gave no visual feedback while the backend provisioned the Matrix user and seeded the workspace (which can take several seconds), making the UI appear frozen. The button now shows a spinner and "Adding…" label, dims, and disables double-submit for the full request duration. (#833)

- **Flow Editor: listener prompt now editable on existing label hand-off edges** — the *Listener prompt* field (the instruction the receiving agent wakes with) was shown when creating a label hand-off but was missing from the edit dialog, leaving it invisible and uneditable on existing edges. The edit dialog now pre-fills the stored prompt verbatim (or generates a default if none is stored) and persists any changes on save. (#834)

- **GitHub channel listeners no longer fire multiple times per event** — when two or more producers handed work to the same consumer over the same label (e.g. two Business Analysts routing to one Dispatcher via `dispatch`), the system generated one trigger per producer and the GitHub poller fired all of them on every matching event, causing the consumer to be woken once per producer. Derived channel triggers are now consolidated into a single listener per unique (events, labelFilter) combination; the contributing producers are recorded for provenance only and have no runtime effect. Existing teams migrate automatically on their next flow edit. (#826)

- **Flow Editor: switch/outcome node cards now grow to contain all outcome ports** — on nodes with 3 or more outcomes, the absolutely-positioned outcome port column was taller than the card, causing ports to spill below the card's bottom border and the layout engine to under-count the node's footprint (misrouting edges). The card now computes a `min-height` from the number of outcome rows so every port sits fully inside the card border. Nodes with 0 or 1 outcomes are unaffected; layout spacing and edge anchoring pick up the corrected height automatically. (#857)

- **Knowledge page: "Propose with AI" and other Knowledge operations no longer intermittently fail with "orchestrator unreachable"** — the LLM skill synthesis helper used a blocking `curl` subprocess call, which froze the orchestrator's event loop for the full LLM round-trip (up to 25 s). During that window every concurrent request from the admin console was rejected with a connection error. The helper is now fully async (non-blocking `fetch` with a 25 s abort signal), keeping the event loop free while synthesis runs. A slow or unreachable LLM now returns the deterministic role-seed fallback instead of a connection error. (#855)

- **Knowledge page Domains panel: domain names now truncate instead of wrapping character-by-character at narrow widths** — at narrow right-rail widths, domain row names broke into a single-character-per-line column because the role tags and action buttons consumed all available space. The name cell now truncates with an ellipsis (full name on hover) and the row wraps controls onto a second line rather than crushing the name. Applies consistently to both domain-list rows and proposal-preview rows. (#856)

- **Knowledge consolidation panel: start gate now correctly accounts for unmatched candidates** — when the consolidation panel computed whether enough work existed to start a migration, it checked only candidates that mapped directly to a confirmed domain and ignored candidates with no domain match. This caused the Start button to remain enabled even when the only remaining items were unmatched candidates that could not be acted on in the current flow. The check now counts all startable work — matched candidates, unconsolidated skills, and unmatched candidates — and the panel transitions correctly to the "nothing left to do" state when only unmatched items remain. (#871)

- **Agent chat: trigger notification cards now render as formatted text** — trigger notification messages (cron alerts, GitHub event notices, throttle warnings) were displayed as raw Markdown text in the agent chat. They are now rendered as sanitized Markdown, so bold text, code spans, lists, and other formatting in trigger prompts and notices appear correctly. (#884)

- **Model provider chip/popover: UI labels now consistently in English** — the provider chip and its popover in the agent chat were displaying some labels in a non-English locale due to a missing i18n override. All strings are now English. (#906)

- **Agent chat right rail: tab overflow restored after collapse/expand cycles** — collapsing and then re-expanding the right rail was losing the tab overflow state, causing the tab bar to clip or misalign when many tabs were present. Tab overflow is now correctly recomputed on each expand. (#924)

- **Agent Chat Sessions: timestamp no longer wraps in the narrow session sidebar** — at narrow sidebar widths, the session timestamp in each session row wrapped onto a second line, breaking the row layout. The timestamp is now prevented from wrapping. (#930)

- **Flow Editor: drag-to-connect preview line now anchors to the grabbed port** — in the Flow Editor, starting a drag from an outcome port (yellow) or a GitHub PR/Issue badge always drew the preview line from the card's centre-right handoff port and coloured it purple, regardless of which port was actually grabbed. The preview line now originates from the port that was dragged and is coloured to match it (amber for outcome and artifact ports, accent colour for the handoff port). The completed connection was not affected — only the visual preview during the drag. (#1030)

- **Flow Editor: dragging an outcome port onto a Human role now opens the delivery dialog** — dropping a typed outcome port (`openIssue`, `openPr`, or `setLabel`) onto a Human role node threw a `role not found` error and refused the connection. Human roles live in a separate registry from agent roles; the outcome-drag path was not checking this before routing. The drop now correctly opens the Agent-to-Person delivery dialog ("reviewer reviews agent's work"), with the review artifact pre-selected for `openIssue`/`openPr` drops. Drops onto agent nodes are unchanged. (#1038)

- **Skills Registry: deleting a global skill no longer fails with a tier validation error** — clicking Delete on a global (pool-tier) skill in the Skills Registry returned `Cannot delete: tier must be one of agent|team|global`. `GET /api/skills` labels global skills with `source: 'pool'`, but the delete endpoint's validator only accepted `agent`, `team`, or `global`. The validator now normalises the `'pool'` alias to `'global'` before checking, so both the confirm-row-delete and the panel Delete button work correctly for global skills. Team-tier and agent-tier deletes are unaffected. (#1045)

- **Agent Chat "About this chat" popover: truncated repo/branch and environment rows now show the full value on hover** — the About section's environment name and repo:branch rows clip with an ellipsis at narrow popover widths but carried no `title` attribute, so a truncated value was unreadable. Both rows now expose a hover tooltip holding the full untruncated text (the repo row includes its `repoPath:branch` when more than one repo is shown). Display only — no behaviour change. (#1835)

- **Agent chat: the "working…" activity indicator now appears during trigger-initiated runs** — the animated indicator only lit when the last transcript entry came from a human, so runs started by a trigger (cron, one-shot, HTTP, GitHub event, or loop) — whose last entry is the agent's own "Trigger fired…" notice — left the chat panel static while the agent worked. The indicator now also turns on when the shared active-sessions poll reports a trigger session for the open room, and clears automatically within ~3 s of the run ending. No new polling or backend. (#1852)

### Changes

- **A new single agent is now minted as a team of one from the start, instead of being converted into one later** — the three fast lanes that used to hand back a standalone agent (**Chat with one assistant**, its repository fast lane, and **Configure an agent manually** when you pick a plain agent role with no team) each now create a `team` record alongside the one member you actually asked for, and grant you that team's Team Admin membership in the same request. You still get exactly the one chattable agent you asked for — its id is unchanged, and the success card and the redirect to chat behave exactly as before — but it now appears in Agent Chat already grouped under its own one-member team (named `<Agent Name> Team`), and its row menu no longer offers **Convert into a team…**, because there is nothing left to convert. That action still applies to an agent created before this change, or one brought in through **Clone** or **Import**, both of which are unaffected. (#2231, completes #2229)

- **Session Log: Edit/Write tool calls now render as a line-level diff instead of raw JSON** — expanding an `Edit` or `Write` tool call in the Session Log panel's collapsible tool blocks used to dump the raw `old_string`/`new_string` (or file content) as one JSON blob, literal `\n` characters and all — unreadable for anything but a one-line change. It now renders as a line-level diff with the same red/green row language the Workspace tab's `DiffView` already uses, plus an old/new line-number gutter neither view had before. `Write` diffs the new file content against nothing (all-additions); `MultiEdit` is unchanged, since it carries an array of edits rather than one before/after pair. The diff is never truncated — the panel's existing collapse/expand control is what keeps a long one out of view by default. (#2189)
- **Session Log: `task_updated`/`task_notification` rows are now readable, not raw JSON** — the two `system_event` subtypes the runtime emits while tracking a launched sub-agent's progress used to fall through the Session Log's generic scalar-only handling, which silently dropped the nested status object and JSON-escaped the markdown report. The collapsed row now shows the task id and its status (`task_updated`) or a one-line snippet of the report (`task_notification`); expanding it renders the status fields as key/value rows, the report as actual markdown (the same renderer chat messages use), and shortens a long absolute output-file path to a workspace-relative reference. Every other `system_event` subtype is unaffected. (#2191)

- **Flow Control removed from the main menu** — the sidebar entry for the **Flow Control** board is gone (it sat in **Agent Setup**, directly under Flows). The page itself, its `/flow-control` route, its context help and everything behind it are unchanged — for now it is reachable only by navigating there directly. This is step 1 of 2: step 2 (#2145) re-ships the board as an installable, team-grantable plugin, at which point it gets a nav entry of its own back. (#2147)

- **A personal GitHub token you never created can no longer take your agents offline** — the personal-credential tier (added earlier) preferred an agent owner's own credential for attributed writes such as commits and pull requests, and *refused the write outright* rather than falling back to a shared identity when the owner had none. That was safe only because nothing had ever recorded an agent's owner; the moment ownership was recorded (above), the rule would have refused every commit, pull request and review in the installation at once, because personal credentials are opt-in and nobody had one yet. The rule now turns on whether the owner actually **uses** personal credentials: an owner who has none takes the shared credential exactly as before, while an owner whose personal credential exists but cannot be used is still refused. The protection keeps its point — a human who *has* a personal credential never has a write silently re-attributed to a shared identity — and stops refusing on behalf of people it was not protecting.

- **"Chat with one assistant" is down to just the form** — two things under the one-assistant page (`/quickstart`) have been removed so it stays focused on the one action it exists for: name, provider, optional repository, create. The footer card **"A single agent is simply a team of one. Grow into a full team →"** is gone — a second call-to-action beneath a four-field form pulled attention away from finishing it — and so is the paragraph under **GitHub Repository URL**, which restated what the field's own `(optional)` label and its placeholder (*"…leave empty for a repo-less agent"*) already say. Nothing takes their place and no empty gap is left behind: the form now ends with its **Create agent & start chatting** button. **Nothing else on the page changed** — the fields, the model-provider hint, the three prerequisite warning cards with their fix links, and the checklist that explains a greyed-out submit button are all untouched, and both submit paths behave exactly as before. The framing and the detail are not lost, only re-homed: the **+ Create** launcher's hero still promises that you can grow one assistant into a team whenever you are ready, and the page's **"?"** still explains what growing does (it creates a *new* team rather than converting this assistant), where a repository URL is normalised to SSH and cloned with the SSH key, and how it can reuse an existing environment and agent. (#2045)

- **The "Add `<role>` role" missing-role suggestion has been removed** — the platform used to compare an Agent Team against the shipped process templates, work out which "loop-closing" role it thought was missing, and offer a one-click **Add `<role>` role**: a warning block on the expanded team card on the Agents page, and extra entries in the team menu in Agent Chat. Both are gone, along with the inference behind them. The suggestion rested on a *guess* — a team never records which process template it was built from, so the source had to be inferred from its label registry and its members' ids. Deliberately conservative, that guess stayed silent on many teams that genuinely had a gap; and where it did fire, "missing" was a claim about a template the team may have diverged from on purpose. A suggestion an operator cannot verify is worse than none, so the capability was retired rather than re-homed. Growing a team is now always an explicit choice: **Add member** (same menu, same route, unchanged) or wiring the role in the **Flow editor**, where a real gap shows up as an unconsumed label or an idle gate instead of an inference. **Nothing you already have changes** — members added through the old button keep working exactly as before, and the Team-Setup wizard, team creation and the Flow Editor's role handling are untouched. For anyone driving the API directly: `POST /api/agents/:teamId/members` with a `{ processTemplateId, roleId }` body is now refused with a clear `400` naming the removal, rather than silently creating a differently-configured member; the `templateId` and free-form bodies are unaffected. (#1959)

- **Agent Chat: "Mark as unread" moved into the row context menu** — the hover-revealed mail icon on the chat rail's agent rows is gone; the action is now the first entry of the row's own context menu (kebab, right-click or `Menu`/`Shift+F10`), above the lifecycle actions and separated from them. It does exactly what the icon did — the row flips back to the blue unread indicator — and it is always listed, greyed out when it does not apply (the agent is already unread, or is disabled/offline/a stub and has no chat to open). Being a local view action, it never depends on your permissions. The rest of the row is unchanged, and the drag grip no longer shifts sideways when you hover a row. (#1938)

- **"Access & Users" nav section renamed to "Access"** — now that human user records are managed end-to-end on the dedicated **Users** page (#1844), the sibling **Administration** entry for `/teams` no longer manages users, so its label drops the "& Users" half that duplicated the item right above it. It now reads **Access** with the subtitle *Teams, roles & assignments*; the breadcrumb and browser tab title for the page read **Access** too. Display-label change only — the `/teams` route, the `/api/teams*` / `/api/admin/*` endpoints and the internal `team` entity are unchanged, and the **Users** page is untouched. Help-panel links and the docs were updated to match. (#1910)

- **Getting Started moved into the Help panel** — the standalone **Getting Started** page and its **Help** entry in the sidebar are gone. Its content — the six concept cards (Agents · Access & Users · Flows · Working Environments · Knowledge · Model Providers), the "where to go next" buttons (*Create a single agent* · *Set up a team* · *Connect a model provider*) and **Take the Tour** — is now the **Overview** face of the header **Help** panel, reachable from any page via the panel's "← Overview" link. Old `/getting-started` bookmarks still work: they open the app home with that Overview showing. (#1874)

- **Page headers unified — one bold title in the top bar, redundant on-page heading removed** — every standard admin-console page previously showed its title twice: a small grey line in the top bar plus a large `<h1>` at the top of the page body. The top-bar title is now the single, **bold** page title (and serves as the page's accessible heading), and the duplicate large on-page heading is gone from the ~20 standard content pages. Each page's **primary action buttons** (e.g. *New Agent* / *Import Agent* on Agents, *New Skill* / *Import skill* on Skills Registry, plus the create/import actions on Role Templates, Working Environments, Model Registry, SSH Keys, GitHub Tokens, Plugins, Teams and Blueprints) have moved up into the top-bar header alongside the Help button; each page's one-line **description** stays in the body. On narrow viewports the title truncates with an ellipsis so the header actions are never pushed off-screen. Bespoke layouts — Agent Chat, the Team-Setup wizard and Quick Start — are unchanged. Behaviour and permissions are unchanged (e.g. the Teams action stays admin-gated). (#1847)

- **Agent Chat empty state: one clear "Create your first agent" call-to-action** — when there are no agents yet, the Agent Chat left-rail empty state now shows a single primary button that opens the unified **`/create`** launcher — the same destination as the sidebar **+ Create** control — instead of the previous three links (`Create a single agent` → `/quickstart`, `Set up a team` → `/team-setup`, and the Getting Started overview → `/getting-started`). This finishes the create-flow consolidation (#1665) for the first-run experience, giving new users one obvious next step. (#1893)

- **Agent Chat header decluttered — "Agent Chat" without the agent id, no "Agents (N)" list heading** — on `/chats/:agentId` the top-bar title (and the browser tab title) now reads simply **Agent Chat** instead of *Agent Chat / `<agentId>`*. Which agent is selected is already shown, highlighted, in the left-rail list, so the id in the header was redundant — and the page header now matches every other page, where the header is just the page name. In the left rail the **Agents (17)** heading above the *All agents* filter is gone as well, so the list starts with the filter and then the agents. Agent selection, filtering and the list itself are unchanged. (#1921)

- **Icon-button and composer hover tooltips unified onto one shared dark tooltip** — every `IconButton` hover tooltip across the console and the Agent-Chat composer's button tooltips now render through a single shared component with one dark/inverted look, so they read identically everywhere (instant on hover **and** keyboard focus, portaled so they are never clipped by a scrolling container, and viewport-clamped). This also **fixes** the composer tooltip that was previously dark-on-dark and unreadable in the **Dark** and **High contrast** themes (it only rendered correctly in Light); dedicated per-theme tooltip colours keep it a readable dark chip in all three themes. No new tooltips are added here — this is the shared foundation; the per-control `title=`→tooltip migration follows separately. (#1850)

- **User management promoted to a dedicated Users page** — human-user administration, previously a **Manage Users** slide-out on the **Access & Users** page, now has its own first-class, routable page at **`/users`** (under **Administration**, System-Admin only). It lists every user in a sortable, filterable table (**Display name · Subject · Email · System Admin · Password**) with a **Create** dialog and a per-row / row-click **Edit** dialog — edit display name, email and System-Admin status, set or clear a login password, and delete with confirmation; the immutable `subject` is shown read-only. The old in-page slide-out is gone; **Access & Users** keeps a **Manage Users** button that links to the new page, so team/access administration and user records are each managed end-to-end in one place. A self-lockout **warning** (never a block) appears if the logged-in admin removes their own System-Admin status or deletes their own record. (#1844)

- **GitHub Event Log: a Type column shows PR vs Issue at a glance** — the **Processed events** table (`/github-events`) gains a **Type** column reading `PR` for pull-request rows and `Issue` for issue rows, so the work-item type is visible without hovering the `#number` link or decoding the Event wording — it is correct even when the Event cell shows a raw technical key (e.g. `pull_request_review.created`). The column sorts and filters like the others; a row with no work item (push / create / delete / release) shows `—`. The writer now also classifies an `issue_comment` on a **pull request** as `PR` rather than `Issue` (newly logged entries only); older entries with no stored type degrade gracefully to `—`. (#1848)
- **Agent Chat model selector aligned with the session toolbar and reordered model-first** — in the Agent Chat toolbar the model/provider selector now renders at the same text size as the neighbouring **Save & Learn** / `…` buttons (no longer visibly larger), and its trigger is wider so a typical `<model> · <provider>` label fits without truncation. The closed trigger now reads **model name first, provider second** (e.g. `claude-opus-4-8 · Claude Enterprise`) — so the running model stays identifiable at a glance; when space is still tight it is the trailing provider name that truncates, never the model. The popover contents and all model/provider switching behaviour are unchanged, and the global assist-model selector in **Settings → Model** is unaffected. (#1845)

- **Triggers panel: enabled state shown by a single toggle in the card header** — each trigger card in the chat right-rail **Triggers** panel previously showed its enabled/disabled state twice — a read-only green check-circle (grey ✕-circle when off) in the header row, plus the actual Enable/Disable toggle switch below the card body. These are now one control: the toggle moved into the header cluster (next to Edit/Delete, in the slot the status icon vacated) with a compact `On`/`Off` label, and the redundant bottom toggle and the separate status icon are gone. A state-aware hover tooltip on the toggle explains what a click will do ("Disable this trigger — it will stop firing" / "Enable this trigger — it will start firing again"), and the in-flight spinner still replaces the toggle while a change is saving. Behaviour is unchanged. (#1849)

- **Font size and theme controls moved to Settings → Display** — the `T — 12px +` font-size stepper has been removed from the agent-chat session toolbar and the light/dark theme toggle has been removed from the top bar. Both controls now live in **Settings → Display** (account menu → Settings), where they sit alongside each other: a Theme selector (Dark / Light / High contrast) and a font-size stepper. Both apply immediately — the font-size updates the active chat transcript without a reload, and the theme switches the console colour scheme instantly. The font-size persists per user as before (`PUT /api/me/preferences` + `agentChat.fontSize.<username>` localStorage fallback); the theme persists in `localStorage` as before. The top bar now shows **Help · account menu** only. (#1806)

- **Reset Session and Clear History moved into a `…` overflow menu in the chat header** — the agent-chat session toolbar no longer shows **Reset Session** and **Clear History** as two always-visible buttons next to **Save & Learn**. The primary **Save & Learn** action stays directly visible; the two rare, destructive actions now live behind a `…` (More session actions) menu to its right. Both actions are unchanged — Clear History still asks for confirmation before deleting, Reset Session keeps its guards, and each still shows its in-progress spinner. The menu is keyboard-accessible (open with Enter/Space, traverse with ↑/↓ and Home/End, close with Escape). (#1810)

- **GitHub Event Log reads as a work log — the work item is now front and centre** — the **Processed events** table (`/github-events`) surfaces which PR or issue each event happened on. Two new columns — **Work item** (`#<number>`, linking to the PR/issue on GitHub) and **Title** (the PR/issue title, truncated with the full text on hover) — sit next to Time, and the text filter now also matches on the work-item number and title. The **Event** cell no longer shows the raw technical event key (e.g. `pull_request.labeled`) beneath the friendly name, and the **Repository** column is de-emphasised (muted short repo name, full `owner/name` on hover) so it no longer dominates each row — it stays sortable and filterable. Existing log entries (7-day retention) simply show `—` for the work item; nothing is backfilled. (#1827)

- **GitHub Event Log follow-up: repository always visible, complete PR links, PR→issue traceability** — three refinements to the **Processed events** table (`/github-events`). (1) The **Repository** column is restored to an early, plainly-visible position (short name, full `owner/name` on hover) so `push` / `create` / `delete` rows — which have no work item or title — are identifiable again instead of reading as empty; those rows also now show the git branch they touched next to the event name. (2) Every pull-request event now shows a linked `#number` **and** the PR title, regardless of how it was handled (delivered, throttled, folded into a digest, or matching no trigger) — previously only delivered PR events were enriched, so the rest showed a bare, unlinked number with an empty title. (3) A PR that resolves an issue now shows a small linked `→ closes #<issue>` next to its number (parsed from the PR body and head branch), giving issue → PR → merge traceability without leaving the page. Existing log entries are not backfilled. (#1833)

- **Sessions panel hover-correlation tip is now a one-time coachmark** — the permanent sentence *"Hover a chat message to highlight the events that produced it…"* no longer sits at the top of the Session Log panel body. The same explanation now appears **once** as a dismissible coachmark with a **Got it** button; the dismissal is remembered per user across sessions, so newcomers see the tip exactly once and power users never see it again. The hover-to-highlight interaction itself is unchanged. (#1808)

- **Sessions/Workspace panel footer removed — shortcut moves to the collapse chevron tooltip** — the permanent *"Press Cmd+Shift+L to show or hide this panel"* footer line at the bottom of the right rail is gone. The keyboard shortcut (`Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+L`) remains discoverable as a tooltip on the panel's collapse (▶) and expand (◀) chevron controls — visible on hover. The shortcut itself is unchanged. (#1809)

- **Send-on-Enter toggle moved from the chat composer to Settings → Behavior** — the `Enter:` drop-down that previously sat below the chat input has been removed from the composer footer. The same setting now lives in **Settings → Behavior** (reached via the account menu → Settings), where it persists per user and applies immediately to the active chat with no reload. The underlying `agentChat.sendKeybinding` preference and send behaviour are unchanged — only the control's location moved. (#1804)

- **Global Triggers (Scheduled Jobs) page retired — trigger management is per-agent only** — the cross-agent **Triggers** aggregation area (`/jobs`) has been removed: the left-nav entry, the route, and the read-only overview page are gone. All trigger management now lives per agent, under the **Triggers** tab in the Agent Chat right rail (and, for flows, the Flows canvas edge dialog). No backend endpoints changed — the page had no dedicated feed, aggregating client-side over `GET /api/agents` and per-agent `GET …/triggers`, all of which are unaffected. Existing triggers and the per-agent editing surface are untouched. (#1817)

- **One wiring vocabulary across the Flow Editor, chat panels and lints** — all wiring surfaces now speak the same eight terms: **Input** (what wakes an agent) · **Outcome** (how a run ends) · **Action** (signal an outcome sets) · **Artifact** (issue / PR / file) · **Consumer** (who reacts) · **Reviewer** (person a delivery goes to) · **Gate** (approval label a person applies) · **Brake** (label that pauses). The three different names for the trigger prompt ("Listener prompt", "Wake prompt override", "Prompt") are unified into a single field: **"Wake instruction — what the consumer is told"** — in every edge dialog and in the Agent-Chat Triggers panel. Lint messages use the same vocabulary ("waits for", "no consumer reacts", "sets a label") instead of the retired emit/listen phrasing. The Team-Setup wizard's inline flow preview now renders the **server-computed graph** — the same edges, styles and legend as the Flows view — instead of a reduced client-side approximation, so what you preview is exactly what materialises. (#1009)

- **Flow Editor: Connections tab retired — controls redistributed across Details and Routing tabs** — the node inspector's tab bar is now **Details · Routing · Triggers** (the former Connections tab is gone). Parent/stage placement fields moved to the Details tab; channel listeners, data-flow inputs, dependencies, derived triggers, and data-flow outputs (Artifacts) moved to the Routing tab. A collapsed **Legacy** section in Routing keeps the emitted-labels editor and GitHub-outputs checkboxes reachable during the transition. Edge-dialog jump links ("Open in Routing" / "Open in Details") are now available for all edge types — channel, label, timer, data-flow, and hierarchy — so clicking any graph edge navigates directly to the relevant inspector row. Draft and live team modes behave identically. No data-model or API changes. (#865)

- **Process Canvas emits now authored at the role/agent level only** — the per-trigger emits editor has been removed from the Triggers panel. Label hand-off edges are now defined exclusively on the role or agent, which was already the canonical location. Existing process graphs are unaffected: any legacy trigger-level emits are automatically lifted to the role level on import or materialisation. (#788)

- **Trigger cards no longer show the raw internal ID** — the grey monospace trigger ID line has been removed from each trigger card in the chat right-rail Triggers panel, reducing visual noise for non-technical readers. The ID is still visible in the trigger edit form, and HTTP-webhook cards continue to show their full webhook path. (#792)

- **Session Log: system event content now visible** — `system_event` rows in the Session Log panel (e.g. `thinking_tokens`) previously rendered as a bare label. They now show an inline summary of their payload (e.g. `thinking_tokens: 1234`) and are click-to-expand to display the full raw JSON — mirroring how other detailed log entries are rendered. No log data format changed; existing persisted logs show content on reload. (#821)

- **Token Usage → Work items: help text on each summary tile** — each of the 8 Work items summary tiles now shows a "?" InfoTip button that, when clicked, reveals a plain-English explanation of the metric. Covered terms include attributed vs. unattributed work items and what each count and cost figure represents. The InfoTip is also touch-reachable (tap to toggle). (#837)

- **Flow Editor: `duplicate-listener` lint warns when a manual trigger duplicates a derived channel listener** — the Flow Editor now surfaces a warning lint when an agent has a manually-authored `github-event` trigger whose event type and label filter are identical to a derived channel listener. This makes the overlap visible before materialising, prompting the operator to remove the redundant trigger. (#826)

- **Session Log: thinking-token entries now collapse into a single self-updating row** — extended thinking runs previously produced one `system_event (thinking_tokens)` row per streamed chunk, filling the Session Log with dozens of near-identical entries during a single response. These are now consolidated in real time: the first chunk opens one collapsible row, subsequent chunks update its counter in place, and the full token total appears once the response completes. Existing historical log entries are unaffected. (#870)

- **Flow Editor: Agent Prompt disclosure removed from node cards** — the expandable agent prompt text block that previously appeared on node inspector cards in the Flow Editor canvas has been removed, reducing card clutter. The agent's full system prompt remains accessible via the **Prompt** tab in the agent chat's right rail. (#873)

- **Flow Editor: Triggers tab retired — Inputs CRUD now matches Triggers** — the separate Triggers tab in the Flow Editor node inspector has been removed. Trigger management has been unified with the Inputs panel, which now supports the same create/edit/delete operations as the Triggers tab previously did. Existing trigger configurations are unaffected. (#866)

- **Flow Editor: four new outcome lints** — the process graph linter now surfaces four additional advisory warnings for structured outcome nodes: `outcome-without-consumer` (an outcome option routes to a label nobody listens on), `switch-with-one-option` (a switch node with a single option — consider a plain label hand-off instead), `same-label-multiple-outcomes` (two or more outcome options emit the same label, making the switch ambiguous), and `human-label-as-outcome` (an outcome emits a `human:*`-prefixed label, which should only be set by a human). All four are advisory — they appear in the lint panel without blocking materialisation. (#830)

- **Flat `emits` retired from role templates; existing templates auto-migrated to structured outcomes** — the flat `emits` list on role and agent configs is no longer the authoring surface for outbound routing in process templates. All built-in role templates have been migrated to use structured `outcomes`. Existing process templates and live teams that still carry flat `emits` are automatically upgraded to the equivalent structured outcomes on import or materialisation — no manual edits required. Authoring new flat `emits` via the API is still accepted for backwards compatibility but the Flow Editor surfaces outcomes exclusively. (#831)

- **Orchestrator baseline SSH key is now optional** — the `docker-compose.yml` no longer requires a baseline SSH key to be mounted into the orchestrator container. Operators who use only HTTPS-based repositories or GitHub token auth can now start the stack without pre-configuring an SSH key file. Existing deployments with an SSH key configured are unaffected. (#903)

- **Agent chat right rail: collapse/expand now matches the left navigation sidebar** — the right rail (Session Log / Workspace Browser) now collapses and expands using the same icon, animation, and interaction pattern as the left navigation sidebar, giving the chat layout a consistent feel on both sides. (#905)

- **Shipped process templates: routing label vocabulary updated** — the built-in `core-sdlc`, `dispatched-sdlc`, and `extended-sdlc` templates and the dispatcher/business-analyst catalog roles have had their routing label tokens renamed for readability and consistency: `ready` → `ready-for-implementation`, `human:dispatch` → `approved-for-dispatch`, `req:refine` / `req:approved` → `needs-refinement` / `requirements-approved`, `design:approved` → `design-approved`, `dispatch` → `dispatched-for-implementation`, and several others. Prompt text, trigger filters, outcome actions, and channel definitions are all updated in step so templates remain internally consistent. **Existing live teams are unaffected** — the rename applies only to the shipped template definitions; newly materialized teams will use the new vocabulary. (#943)
- **Process graph: gate and brake classification is now flag-driven** — whether a routing label acts as a human approval gate or a loop brake is now determined by its config flags (`humanGate: true` / `brake: true` in the label registry, `gate: 'human'` on a channel), not by the label's name. The former conventions — the `human:*` prefix and the hardcoded `hold`/`needs:human` brake set — are retired as a classification source. Labels flagged `brake: true` can now be applied by agents without triggering a lint error; only `humanGate`-flagged labels remain forbidden as agent emits. (#939)

- **Agent and environment config-write endpoints now enforce team-ownership authorization** — previously, any authenticated session (including a Team Member from an unrelated team) could write to agent, environment, and MCP credential endpoints without restriction. Config-write and CRUD endpoints now require the caller to be a Team Admin of the resource's owning team, or a System/Environment Admin. Affected surfaces: agent create/update/delete/clone/import, trigger CRUD, environment create/update/delete, MCP credential create/delete, and agent prompt/model/provider writes (which were previously only visibility-gated). Re-parenting a resource to a different team additionally requires Team Admin rights on the destination team. Single-operator deployments using the default admin account are unaffected. Denied attempts are recorded in the audit trail with outcome `denied`. (#1032)

- **GitHub Event Log: Outcomes column now shows the label that actually triggered each delivery** — when a multi-label change on GitHub is collapsed into a single event (GitHub's Events API picks one label name arbitrarily), the **Event** cell no longer shows the arbitrary collapsed label name — e.g. `Issue labeled (ux)` — when the event woke one or more agents. The label that each subscriber actually matched is now shown per outcome chip: e.g. `delivered (ready) → developer`. For subscribers with no `labelFilter` (any-label listeners) no label is shown. For rows where no agent was listening (`noMatchingTrigger: true`) the collapsed event-level label is still shown next to the event name, since there is no per-subscriber attribution to report. Log rows written before this change show no label on outcomes rather than a potentially misleading one. (#1846)

- **Access is now decided by Agent Team membership, not by the separate access-scope layer** — "who can see and change which agents" used to be answered by a second grouping layer sitting beside the squads you actually work in: a *Team* access scope that owned agents, environments and credentials, and that a person held a membership in. That layer is being collapsed, so that the **Agent Team** — the squad that works a repo — is simultaneously the access boundary (epic #2076). This release re-points the **agent** gates: a team agent is its own boundary, a team member is bounded by the team it belongs to, and a **standalone agent is now private to the person who created it** rather than visible to everyone in its access scope. A Team Admin manages the agents of the teams they administer; a Team Member reads and chats and changes nothing; the person who owns a standalone agent manages that one. A **System Admin still sees and manages everything** — that is unchanged and deliberate. Two consequences worth knowing. A resource carrying **no** team at all is no longer quietly treated as belonging to the default team: it is invisible to everyone except a System Admin, so an unowned leftover fails closed instead of being shared with whoever happens to hold a membership named `default`. And **model providers, credentials, working environments and MCP servers are not re-pointed yet** — they keep their access-scope ownership until a later phase adds a shared tier for them, so nobody loses a provider or a repository in this release. **Single-operator installations are unaffected end to end**: with per-user authorization off (the default), every principal is the operator and every gate is a no-op. (#2078)

### Breaking Changes

- **BriefingPack orchestrators removed** — the platform used to carry a second, older way of running work alongside Agent Teams and flows: an **Orchestrator** agent bound to a directory of **BriefingPacks** (`brief.md` mission files), which scanned that directory, wrote one `bp-instance` "mission agent" per pack, and routed mission events between them. It predates the Flow/Team model, was never fully functional, and is not supported. It is being removed (epic #1961); this release removes its **orchestrator-service and console-API half** (#1962), its **admin console UI** (#1964), the **Flows page's BriefingPack mode** (#1965) and the **`bp-explorer` service together with all of its build and deployment wiring** (#1963). The agent image's mission-dispatch mode (#1967) is removed in this release too (see below); one thing still follows in the remaining phases of that epic: the Agents page (#1930), which is where a leftover Orchestrator's card — including a **Sync BPs** button whose endpoint this release already removed — is still rendered.

  **What no longer works.** You can no longer create an agent with the role `orchestrator` — `POST /api/agents` (and the internal `POST /internal/agents`) now rejects it with *"Invalid role"*, and the role `bp-instance` is never written by anything. The mission-scanning, stub-creation and mission-event endpoints are gone: `POST /api/agents/:id/sync-bps`, the `/api/bp-explorer/*` proxy, `POST /internal/orchestrators/:id/sync-bps`, `POST /internal/orchestrators/:id/claim-tick`, `POST /internal/agents/:id/claim` and the retired `POST /internal/bp-events` stub. The `briefingsPath` field is no longer accepted on agent create/update or on a blueprint.

  **The Flows page no longer lists BriefingPack flows.** Its left list shows your live Teams and your Drafts, and nothing else. The read-only BriefingPack viewer is gone with it, and so is editing a `brief.md`'s front matter from the flow editor — the pack files are now only ever *read*. `GET /api/flows/graph?workspace=<path>` no longer has a BriefingPack branch: the parameter is simply ignored and the endpoint answers with the live graph, exactly as it does for any other unrecognised query parameter. An old bookmark carrying `?flow=<orchestratorId>` drops the stale parameter and lands on the page's default state instead of an empty pane, and a remembered BriefingPack selection is not restored. **Flow authoring itself is untouched** — layout, wiring, edges, the edge dialogs and materialization all behave exactly as before.

  **Importing a BriefingPack hierarchy as a flow draft is deliberately kept**, because it is the migration path off the model — and it now reads the `brief.md` files directly instead of going through `bp-explorer`, which is what allowed that service to be deleted in this same release (#1963). It is no longer reached by selecting an Orchestrator in a list; it is an explicit **Import BriefingPacks…** action in the Flows page header, next to the ordinary flow import, and it asks which agent's workspace holds the packs and which mission directory to read. The import produces the same draft it always did, with one exception worth knowing if you hold such a repository: packs are now read from the **checked-out tree only**. `bp-explorer` also overlaid unmerged `agent/*` branches and local worktrees (`.worktrees/`, `.claude/worktrees/`), taking two things from them that both end up in the draft — a pack's `brief.md` **body** (only when that copy was further along; it feeds the drafted role prompt) and its **artifacts** (whenever that copy listed any, *regardless* of how far along it was; they feed the role's inputs and outputs). The platform no longer creates those branches — the claim endpoint went in the first phase of this removal — so before importing, **merge or delete any stale `agent/*` branches and leave no stale worktree copy**. Deleting matters as much as merging: a branch already at or behind `main` has nothing to merge, but its artifacts list would still have been picked up by the old importer. Once the checked-out tree is the whole truth, the draft is identical. Two things changed for anyone calling it directly: `POST /api/process/drafts/import-briefings` now takes `{ agentId, path }` — a workspace-relative path resolved through the same containment check every other workspace endpoint uses — instead of a bare absolute `workspace` path, and a mission directory that is missing, empty or carries no readable pack ids now answers with a sentence explaining which, rather than a generic upstream error. (#1965)

  **The `bp-explorer` service is gone, and so is every trace of it in the build and deployment machinery.** It was a separate Python service that read BriefingPack directories and served them over HTTP — built as its own container image, deployed as its own Kubernetes workload in every environment, and started by every local and cloud bring-up script. With the packs no longer driving anything and the surviving importer reading `brief.md` files directly, it has no reason to exist. Removed from `docker-compose.yml`, from the Kubernetes base and the `test`/`preprod` overlays, from `kind-up.sh` / `gke-up.sh` / `render-check.sh` / `deploy/gcp/vm-stack-setup.sh`, and from the deploy workflow, which no longer builds or pushes the `agent-studio-bp-explorer` image. A local `docker compose up` and a `kind-up.sh` run bring up the stack with no missing-service error and no dangling rollout wait.

  **An existing cluster is cleaned up for you.** `kubectl apply -k` does not prune, so an environment first deployed before this release would have kept the old Deployment and Service running against the shared workspace volume indefinitely. Both up scripts and the deploy workflow now delete `deploy/bp-explorer` and `svc/bp-explorer` on every apply (`--ignore-not-found`, so it is a no-op everywhere else) — the workload is reaped on the next deploy rather than orphaned, and there is nothing to do by hand. The workspace volume itself is untouched: the components that still mount it are unaffected.

  **`~/.gitconfig` is no longer a prerequisite for starting the stack.** This service was the only thing that bind-mounted it, and it refused to start when the file was missing — which is why the setup guides told you to create one before your first `make up`. No compose service mounts it any more, so that step is gone; set a global git identity if you want one for your own commits, but the stack no longer cares.

  **One API endpoint went with the service.** `GET /api/agents/:agentId/workspace/dirs` — a directory-only listing that proxied the service's `/api/workspace/tree`, and the one consumer of `bp-explorer` that had nothing to do with BriefingPacks — is removed as dead code, together with `DirectoryPicker.jsx`, the orchestrator-role directory picker that was its only caller and which nothing in the console imported. Should a directory picker ever be wanted again, it should be re-implemented locally rather than as a proxy: the sibling `GET /api/agents/:agentId/workspace/tree` already walks the agent workspace directly and is unchanged. (#1963)

  **What operators should do about leftover configurations.** Nothing is deleted for you and there is no migration step: an existing `orchestrator` or `bp-instance` under `/workspace/agents/<id>/` keeps its **`config.json` exactly as it is** — nothing on the upgrade path rewrites it. (The one thing that is not promised: an already-materialized leftover still shares the ordinary workspace migrations, so its generated `CLAUDE.md` may be rebuilt like any other agent's. Its configuration, which is what you would read or delete, is untouched.) On the next start the orchestrator **skips** each of them — no error, no crash, and exactly one explanatory log line per configuration naming the role and this epic, so the skip is findable rather than silent. They are also listed in the boot summary marked *unsupported, skipped*. Every other agent, team and flow starts as before.

  **A mission container that is still running at upgrade time is stopped and not restarted.** The orchestrator already force-removes every agent container at boot before respawning the ones whose configuration is enabled; a skipped configuration is simply not respawned, so its container goes away with the upgrade and nothing brings it back. In-flight mission work is *not* drained first — if a mission container is doing something you care about, let it finish before upgrading.

  When you no longer need a leftover configuration, delete the agent's directory under `/workspace/agents/`. Nothing in the platform reads `briefingsPath`, `parentOrchestratorId`, `bpId`, `bpSlug` or `bpRelPath` any more, so leaving them costs nothing but disk. (#1962)

  **The agent container now has exactly one operating mode.** Every agent image carried a second one: given `--bp-hash`, it looked for a `brief.md` in the repository's `artifacts/briefings` tree, assembled a mission prompt from that pack plus a LoopScript and any MentorScripts, invoked Claude once, then committed and pushed. That mode is gone, together with the 711-line `dispatch-bp.py` that drove it, the `container-lifecycle.py` helper that created the mission containers, the `sase_lib.py` pack-scanning library both of them used, and the seeded `artifacts/briefings/` fixture tree. **The conversational path is untouched** — an agent started from the new image joins its room and responds exactly as before. Three things are worth knowing if you drive the entrypoint by hand. Its argument parser now accepts **no arguments at all** (`--agent-id`, `--bp-hash`, `--mode`, `--repo-url` and `--branch` all belonged to the removed mode), so passing one fails with *unrecognized arguments* rather than being quietly ignored — the orchestrator has always invoked it with no arguments. Its **SIGTERM** handling no longer commits and pushes partial work before exiting 124; the surviving mode has no bound `/repo` to commit to, so a shutdown signal simply winds the listener down and exits 0. And a leftover `brief.md` in an upgraded agent's workspace is now **ignored** rather than read — nothing looks for one. Also removed: the BriefingPack **claim loop**, a 30-second poll in every orchestrator-role container that asked for newly-dispatched packs and offered to claim them; both its endpoint and the `orchestrator` role itself were already gone, so it could never fire. (#1967)
  **The base instruction file no longer describes a world that was deleted.** The instruction file baked into every agent image (`agent-image/CLAUDE.md`) opened by telling the agent it was *executing a BriefingPack*, and went on to prescribe a `bp:`-prefixed commit format, a branch created by a dispatcher, LoopScript phases to follow strictly and sequentially, and a CRP (Consultation Request Protocol) file before any blocking decision. It now describes the supported model instead: an agent works **standalone or as a member of a team**, is started either by a person writing to it or by a **trigger** (a schedule, a webhook, a GitHub event such as a label being applied, or a loop), and hands back an **outcome** — a commit, a pull request, a comment, a registered artifact, or the label that wakes the next agent, with nothing downstream picking the work up if a run ends without one. The commit convention becomes *match the repository you are working in* rather than a platform-wide format; the one hard-coded project rule (a read-only `10-legacy/` directory) is replaced by a pointer to the Working Environment's additional context, which is where a project-specific rule belongs. **No agent behaviour changes** — and that is worth stating precisely, because the opposite was assumed when this was scoped out: the baked file lands at `/opt/ace/CLAUDE.md`, which no runtime path reads. The instructions an agent actually reads at session start are assembled per agent by the orchestrator into that agent's own workspace, and those never mentioned BriefingPacks. What the baked copy is for is describing the container's own tooling — `gh-as-agent`, `aee-artifact`, `aee-emit`, `knowledge`, `usage`, `ticket.json` — to whoever opens it, and the toolchain base-contract check asserts it is present. So the gain is not a behavioural one: it is that a reader is no longer instructed to follow a protocol that does not exist. (#1969)

- **Default `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` renamed from `vgh-claw` to `agent-studio`** — the legacy customer name has been removed from the codebase. The compose project prefix (used for all container and named-volume names) now defaults to `agent-studio`. **This orphans your existing named volumes:** compose will create fresh, empty volumes under the new prefix, so the skill pool, agent workspaces, chat history and Claude `--resume` state will appear "lost" until you migrate. Package names (`agent-studio-admin-console`, `agent-studio-admin-api`), the poller/wizard `User-Agent` strings, and the orchestrator git-author email (`orchestrator@agent-studio.local`) changed too, but those are cosmetic. Choose one upgrade path before running `make up` on an existing stack:

  **Option 1 — keep your old volumes (zero-risk, recommended for existing deployments).** Pin the old prefix in `.env` so nothing moves:
  ```bash
  echo 'COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=vgh-claw' >> .env   # or your existing custom prefix
  ```

  **Option 2 — adopt the new prefix and migrate volume data.** Podman has no in-place volume rename on older versions, so copy each volume via a helper container while the stack is stopped:
  ```bash
  make down
  OLD=vgh-claw; NEW=agent-studio          # use your actual old prefix
  for v in conduwuit-db agent-pool-workspace claude-workspace claude-home agent-control-plane; do
    podman volume create "${NEW}_$v"
    podman run --rm -v "${OLD}_$v":/from:ro -v "${NEW}_$v":/to \
      docker.io/library/busybox sh -c 'cp -a /from/. /to/'
  done
  # If AEE_PER_AGENT_VOLUME=1, repeat for each ${OLD}_agent-<id> volume
  # (list them with: podman volume ls | grep "^.*${OLD}_agent-")
  make up
  # After verifying the stack is healthy, remove the old volumes:
  #   podman volume rm ${OLD}_conduwuit-db ${OLD}_agent-pool-workspace ${OLD}_claude-workspace ${OLD}_claude-home ${OLD}_agent-control-plane
  ```
  A live-deploy checkout previously kept at `~/Projects/e-vgh-claw` can be renamed for tidiness, but the directory name does not drive the compose prefix — only `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` does. Fresh installs need no action. (#913)

- **Token Usage page now restricted to System Admins** — the `/token-usage` route and all five usage API endpoints (`/api/usage/summary`, `/summary/export`, `/work-items`, `/work-items/export`, `/work-items/:owner/:repo/:number`) now return 403 for any caller who is not a System Admin or environment admin. The nav entry is hidden for Team Members and Team Admins. (#810)

- **Credential and Model Provider visibility now requires Team Admin role** — SSH keys, GitHub tokens, and Model Providers are no longer visible to plain Team Members in the Working Environment editor's credential pickers, or on the respective admin pages. Only Team Admins (and System Admins) can view and manage these resources. (#803, #806)

- **Removed trigger-level emits API endpoints** — `GET /api/agents/:id/triggers/:triggerId/emit-suggestions` and `PUT /api/agents/:id/triggers/:triggerId/emits` have been removed. Use the agent-level `emits` field (via `PUT /api/agents/:id`) instead. Any `emits` field supplied to `PUT /api/agents/:id/triggers/:triggerId` is silently stripped. (#788)

- **Live teams: labels are no longer classified as gates or brakes by name alone** — on live teams without a bundle registry, labels whose names match the old conventions (`human:*` prefix, `hold`, `needs:human`) no longer carry implicit gate or brake semantics. Classification now reads the `humanGate` / `brake` flags that the platform writes into the label registry at materialisation time. Teams using custom label names that were previously classified by name convention will need to re-materialise (or update the team agent's `labels[]` config directly) to restore gate or brake behavior. Built-in templates (`core-sdlc`, `dispatched-sdlc`, `extended-sdlc`) ship the flags and are unaffected. (#939)
